'''Daniel Lewis Cox''' (born August 9, 1974) is an American politician and lawyer, and [[perennial candidate]]. He was a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] member of the [[Maryland House of Delegates]], representing the [[Maryland Legislative District 4|fourth district]] from 2019 to 2023.

Cox was the unsuccessful Republican nominee in the [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election]].<ref name="GovernorVictory">{{cite news |last1=Gomez |first1=Henry |title=Trump-backed election denier Dan Cox wins Maryland's GOP primary for governor |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-election/trump-backed-election-denier-dan-cox-wins-marylands-gop-primary-govern-rcna38725 |access-date=July 19, 2022 |work=[[NBC News]] |date=July 19, 2022}}</ref> In 2024, Cox unsuccessfully ran for the U.S. House of Representatives in [[Maryland's 6th congressional district]], losing to former state delegate [[Neil Parrott]] in the Republican primary election.<ref name="Cox2024" /><ref name="ParrottPrimary">{{cite news |last1=Shepherd |first1=Katie |last2=Asbury |first2=Nicole |last3=Portnoy |first3=Jenna |title=April McClain Delaney to face Neil Parrott in Md. 6th District in Nov. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/05/14/maryland-primary-election-6th-district/ |access-date=May 15, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=May 14, 2024 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613000830/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/05/14/maryland-primary-election-6th-district/ |archive-date=June 13, 2024}}</ref> Cox is the Republican nominee for governor in the [[2026 Maryland gubernatorial election]].<ref name="tops" />

Cox is characterized as a [[Far-right politics in the United States|far-right]] politician.<ref>{{cite news |last=Janesch |first=Sam |date=June 21, 2022 |title=Pennsylvania's far-right, Trump-endorsed candidate was nominated for governor. Is Maryland's next? |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/06/21/pennsylvanias-far-right-trump-endorsed-candidate-was-nominated-for-governor-is-marylands-next/ |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220622185848/https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-dan-cox-mastriano-20220621-y6pj5uhmdzcr5gcwm4x7gqfyiu-story.html |archive-date=June 22, 2022 |access-date=July 20, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]]}}</ref><ref name="DGA Cox" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=July 19, 2022 |title=Dan Cox, a far-right Trump loyalist, wins Maryland's Republican primary for governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2022/07/19/us/maryland-midterm-primary-elections#dan-cox-a-far-right-trump-loyalist-wins-marylands-republican-primary-for-governor |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 20, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> An [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|election denier]], he has continuously espoused the theory that the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 U.S. presidential election]] was fraudulent.<ref name="GovernorVictory" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cillizza |first1=Chris |title=Another election denier just won a big GOP primary |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/07/20/politics/dan-cox-maryland-republican-governor-election/index.html |access-date=October 18, 2022 |work=[[CNN]] |date=July 20, 2022}}</ref><ref name="Simonaire" />

==Early life and education==
Cox was born in Washington, D.C., on August 9, 1974,<ref name="msa">{{cite web |date=January 26, 2023 |title=Daniel L. Cox, Maryland State Delegate |url=https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/mdmanual/06hse/former/html/msa18012.html |access-date=February 5, 2023 |website=Maryland Manual On-Line |publisher=[[Maryland State Archives]]}}</ref> as one of ten children to Gary Cox. His father was a pastor.<ref name="Upended">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Dan Cox upended the status quo in Maryland's Republican primary. Where will the election for governor take him? |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/10/15/dan-cox-upended-the-status-quo-in-marylands-republican-primary-where-will-the-election-for-governor-take-him/ |access-date=October 15, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=October 15, 2022}}</ref><ref name="wedding">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-star-democrat-gilliard-cox-11-aug-1/200206635/ |title=Gilliard-Cox |date=August 11, 1996 |newspaper=[[The Star Democrat]] |page=3C |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=June 24, 2026}}{{Open access}}</ref> His father named him after the biblical prophet, [[Daniel (biblical figure)|Daniel]].<ref name="Upended" /> Cox initially lived in [[Takoma Park, Maryland]], but his family moved north to Frederick County after his father took a job there as a minister. Cox grew up on a farm near [[Taneytown, Maryland]].<ref name="fnp interview 2016">{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |title=Cox leans on inspiration from past, looks to country's future in congressional bid |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_2016/cox-leans-on-inspiration-from-past-looks-to-countrys-future-in-congressional-bid/article_75628ec7-9ade-5d50-a0dc-2faa8aeb9e63.html |access-date=November 27, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=October 6, 2016}}</ref>

Cox attended the Wellspring Christian Family Schools, a faith-based home-school organization founded by his father, as a child.<ref name="Upended" /> He attended [[Mount St. Mary's University]] from 1992 to 1995 and later attended the [[University of Maryland Global Campus]], where in 2002 he earned a bachelor's degree in government and politics.<ref name="Upended" /> He attended [[Regent University School of Law]], earning a [[Juris Doctor|J.D.]] degree with distinction in 2006.<ref name="Homeschooling" />

==Career== 
In 2006, he became a member of the [[Maryland State Bar Association]]<ref name="msa" /> and was a sole practitioner outside of [[Emmitsburg, Maryland]].<ref name="fnp interview 2016" /> Cox was also a member of the [[Alliance Defending Freedom]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rose Madden |first1=Mary |title=Frederick County School Board faces a lawsuit for passing a policy that protects trans students |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2017-10-05/frederick-county-school-board-faces-a-lawsuit-for-passing-a-policy-that-protects-trans-students |access-date=September 1, 2022 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=October 5, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.splcenter.org/fighting-hate/extremist-files/group/alliance-defending-freedom|title=Alliance Defending Freedom|website=Southern Poverty Law Center|access-date=September 1, 2022}}</ref>

Before getting involved with Maryland politics, Cox taught as a high school teacher from 1995 to 2005 at Walkersville Christian Family Schools.<ref name="Homeschooling">{{cite news |last1=Jamison |first1=Peter |title=The Christian home-schooling world that shaped Dan Cox |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/11/03/dan-cox-christian-homeschooling-maryland/ |access-date=November 3, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=November 3, 2022}}</ref> He also served as a [[Captain (United States O-3)|Captain]] in the [[Civil Air Patrol]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dress |first1=Brad |title=Five things to know about Dan Cox, Maryland's GOP nominee for governor |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3567534-five-things-to-know-about-dan-cox-marylands-gop-nominee-for-governor/ |access-date=September 6, 2022 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=July 20, 2022}}</ref>

==Political involvement==
Cox says he has been active in politics since 1980, when he campaigned with his parents for [[Ronald Reagan]]. He also worked on the [[1996 United States presidential election|1996 presidential campaign]] of [[Alan Keyes]] and as an aide to former [[U.S. Representative]] [[Roscoe Bartlett]].<ref name="Nirappil" /> He was the Republican nominee for [[Dorchester County, Maryland|Dorchester County]] Circuit Court Clerk in 2006, losing the race to Democratic nominee Michael L. Baker.<ref name="2006 general">{{cite web |title=Dorchester County, Maryland - General Election Returns 2006 |url=https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/mdmanual/36loc/do/elect/general/do2006.html#clerk |website=Maryland Manual On-Line |publisher=Maryland State Archives |access-date=February 7, 2020 |date=September 29, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Official 2006 Gubernatorial General Election results for Dorchester County |url=https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2006/results/general/local_office_Dorchester_County.html |website=elections.maryland.gov |publisher=Maryland State Board of Elections |access-date=February 7, 2020}}</ref> Cox ran on a platform that included establishing a division to help fathers gain visitation, ensuring mothers received child support, and refusing to issue licenses for [[same-sex marriage in Maryland|same-sex marriages]], which were not legal in Maryland at the time.<ref name="Upended" /> From 2007 to 2009, he was the President of the Town Commission of [[Secretary, Maryland]].<ref name="msa" />

===2016 congressional campaign===
[[File:Dan Cox MD08 debate (30227474111).jpg|thumb|Cox speaking at a debate, 2016]]
{{Main|2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Maryland#District 8}}
On February 1, 2016, Cox filed to run in [[2016 United States House of Representatives elections in Maryland#District 8|2016]] in [[Maryland's 8th congressional district]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2016 Presidential Primary Election State Candidates List |url=https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2016/primary_candidates/gen_cand_lists_2016_3_00808.html |website=elections.maryland.gov |publisher=[[Maryland State Board of Elections]] |access-date=November 27, 2021}}</ref>

During the Republican primary, Cox was described as being the most conservative candidate in the Republican primary race.<ref name="Nirappil" /> He campaigned on imposing a 10 percent flat tax for incomes over $36,000 and eliminating payroll taxes, strengthening gun ownership rights, abolishing the [[Internal Revenue Service]], and reducing funding and programs for the departments of [[United States Department of Commerce|Commerce]], [[United States Department of Education|Education]], [[United States Department of Energy|Energy]], and [[Housing and Urban Development]].<ref name="fnp interview 2016" /> He supported [[Ted Cruz]] in the [[2016 Republican Party presidential primaries]].<ref name="Nirappil" /> Cox pledged to join the [[Freedom Caucus]], a group of [[Tea Party movement|tea party]] supporters, if elected.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Peck |first1=Louis |title=Raskin Captures Democratic Nod to Succeed Van Hollen in 8th Congressional District |url=https://bethesdamagazine.com/2016/04/26/raskin-captures-democratic-nod-to-succeed-van-hollen-in-8thh-congressional-district/ |access-date=September 1, 2022 |work=[[Bethesda Magazine]] |date=April 26, 2016}}</ref>

In September 2016, Cox claimed that "far-left sign Nazis" were stealing campaign signs he posted alongside state highways, and posted on Twitter that he had urinated on the signs as a deterrent to prevent theft.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |title=Political Notes: Stinky security measure aimed at sign bandits |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/political-notes-stinky-security-measure-aimed-at-sign-bandits/article_b7133bef-c386-5776-b3d9-66dad55ce22f.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=September 29, 2016}}</ref>

Cox won the Republican primary with 44.4 percent of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jones |first1=Paige |title=Raskin to battle Cox for 8th District seat in November |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_2016/raskin-to-battle-cox-for-8th-district-seat-in-november/article_7f68bd42-45b3-5968-8f4c-ff9738e8aad2.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=April 26, 2016}}</ref> In the general election, he was defeated by Democratic nominee and state senator [[Jamie Raskin]] with 34.2 percent of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|title=Dan Cox (Maryland)|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Dan_Cox_(Maryland)|access-date=February 7, 2020|website=[[Ballotpedia]]|language=en}}</ref><ref name="2016results">{{cite web|date=December 9, 2016|title=Official 2016 Presidential General Election results for Representative in Congress|url=https://elections.maryland.gov/elections/2016/results/general/gen_results_2016_4_00808.html|access-date=February 7, 2020|website=elections.maryland.gov|publisher=Maryland State Board of Elections}}</ref>

===Maryland House of Delegates===
[[File:GOP Caucus Reception - 49617550556.jpg|thumb|alt=Delegate Dan Cox with Governor Larry Hogan|Cox with Governor [[Larry Hogan]], who declined to endorse Cox during the 2022 Maryland gubernatorial election.]]
In February 2018, Cox announced that he would run for the Maryland House of Delegates in District 4. He ran on a platform that included [[deregulation|cutting regulations]], increasing immigration enforcement, and supporting gun rights.<ref name="Cox2018">{{cite news |last1=Masters |first1=Kate |title=Dan Cox |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/dan-cox/article_1901f61a-2e8e-5322-a30c-ebc766528f72.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=February 24, 2018}}</ref> He campaigned on a platform that included lowering taxes, expanding [[Interstate 270 (Maryland)|Interstate 270]], and supporting [[charter school]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Ryan |title=District 4 Republicans bring focus on opioids, education |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/district-4-republicans-bring-focus-on-opioids-education/article_d817f53f-d808-5401-9054-f9b9ec5891ac.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=October 22, 2018}}</ref> In November 2018, [[electioneering]] complaints were filed against Cox after he recorded a video of himself within 100 feet of an [[Early voting#United States|early voting]] center in [[Thurmont, Maryland|Thurmont]], which is prohibited by Maryland election law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Electioneering complaints filed during early voting against Cox |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/electioneering-complaints-filed-during-early-voting-against-cox/article_5fadae5c-6708-57c9-a01c-6e6394ee16e1.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=November 2, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=State may consider complaints against Delegate-elect Dan Cox |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/state-may-consider-complaints-against-delegate-elect-dan-cox/article_afaaee9d-8f59-5a60-b081-112aef0a3c44.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=December 4, 2018}}</ref>

Cox was sworn into the Maryland House of Delegates on January 9, 2019. He was a member of the Judiciary Committee, serving on its family law and public safety subcommittees from 2019 to 2020 and its family and juvenile law and civil law and procedure subcommittees from 2021 to 2023.<ref name="msa" /> In his first term, Cox filed 84 bills,<ref name="Upended" /> only two of which passed (both of which were introduced in 2019), and attached amendments to others.<ref name="Backbench">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Dan Cox was a backbench Md. lawmaker. Then the pandemic hit. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/14/dan-cox-nominee-maryland/ |access-date=September 14, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=September 14, 2022}}</ref>

From 2018 to 2021, Cox served as the secretary of the [[Frederick County, Maryland|Frederick County]] Republican Central Committee.<ref name="msa" /> During the [[2020 United States presidential election]], he served as a Frederick County co-chair for the state's Trump Victory Leadership County team.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dan Cox |url=https://www.frederickgop.org/copy-of-cindy-trout |website=Frederick County GOP |access-date=October 12, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210322140920/https://www.frederickgop.org/copy-of-cindy-trout |archive-date=March 22, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Uncapher |first1=Mark |title=Maryland Trump County Leaders Announced |url=https://redmaryland.com/2020/01/maryland-trump-county-leaders-announced/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=RedMaryland.com |date=January 18, 2020 |archive-date=November 23, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211123220515/https://redmaryland.com/2020/01/maryland-trump-county-leaders-announced/}}</ref>

===Involvement in the January 6 United States Capitol attack===
In November 2020, Cox said that he was part of a Republican legal team observing the count of mail-in ballots in [[Philadelphia]] during the [[2020 United States presidential election]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shwe |first1=Elizabeth |title=Leading Md. Republicans Largely Align With Trump on Election Outcome |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2020/11/10/leading-md-republicans-largely-align-with-trump-on-election-outcome/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=November 10, 2020}}</ref> After [[Donald Trump]] lost the 2020 presidential election, Cox has repeatedly endorsed Trump's false claims of a stolen election and called for a "forensic audit" of the 2020 election results,<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/maryland-governor-elections-2022-candidates/ |title=Trump-backed Dan Cox projected to win Maryland GOP primary for governor |first=Aaron |last=Navarro |date=July 20, 2022 |publisher=CBS News}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Fleming |first1=Leonard |title=Maryland gubernatorial candidates address controversial pasts and views |url=https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/politics/election-maryland/maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-address-controversial-pasts-and-views/ |access-date=September 23, 2022 |work=[[WDCW]] |date=September 23, 2022 |archive-date=September 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220923012845/https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/politics/election-maryland/maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-address-controversial-pasts-and-views/}}</ref> later calling for an audit of the 2020 elections in Maryland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Cox, Pro-Trump Delegate, Launches Gubernatorial Bid with Call for Election "Audit" |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/07/05/cox-pro-trump-delegate-launches-gubernatorial-bid-with-call-for-election-audit/ |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=July 5, 2021}}</ref>

Cox helped arrange for buses to take constituents to the "[[January 6 Trump rally|Save America March]]" in Washington, D.C., on January 6, 2021; the rally preceded the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack|violent attack on the U.S. Capitol]]. During the rally, Cox sent a [[Twitter|tweet]] disparaging Vice President [[Mike Pence]], writing "Pence is a traitor."<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.fox5dc.com/news/trump-endorses-maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-who-called-pence-a-traitor-during-jan-6-riot-rips-hogan |title=Trump endorses Maryland gubernatorial candidate who called Pence 'a traitor' during Jan. 6 riot; rips Hogan |date=November 22, 2021 |work=[[WTTG|FOX 5 DC]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/01/07/del-cox-arranged-buses-to-violent-trump-rally-calls-vice-president-a-traitor/|title=Del. Cox Arranged Buses to Violent Trump Rally, Calls Vice President a 'Traitor'|first=Hannah|last=Gaskill|date=January 7, 2021|access-date=January 11, 2021|work=Maryland Matters}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Kaleigh |date=November 1, 2022 |title=Meet The Midterm Candidates Who Attended The Jan. 6 Rally |url=https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/meet-the-midterm-candidates-who-attended-the-jan-6-rally/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221101101407/https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/meet-the-midterm-candidates-who-attended-the-jan-6-rally/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=November 1, 2022 |access-date=November 6, 2022 |website=FiveThirtyEight |language=en-US}}</ref> After receiving backlash, Cox tweeted and retweeted false claims blaming [[Antifa (United States)|antifa]] for the attack on the Capitol, and expressed his support for [[Enrique Tarrio]], the leader of the [[Proud Boys]], an extremist group with nationalist, neofascist and self-proclaimed Western-chauvinist views.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=In Wake of Cox's 'Traitor' Tweet, Jones Says Situation Is Being 'Addressed' |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/01/15/in-wake-of-coxs-traitor-tweet-jones-says-situation-has-been-addressed/ |access-date=October 23, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=January 15, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Fact check: Men who stormed Capitol identified by Reuters are not undercover Antifa as posts claim |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/uk-factcheck-capitol-mob-antifa-undercov-idUSKBN29E0QO/ |access-date=April 14, 2021 |work=[[Reuters]] |date=January 9, 2021 |language=en}}</ref> Cox later said in June 2022 that his Twitter post was "his way of expressing his disappointment and not a personal attack on the vice president."<ref name="Banner bio">{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |date=June 29, 2022 |title=Republican Dan Cox is leaning on Trump's support to lead him to Maryland's governorship |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/republican-dan-cox-is-leaning-on-trumps-support-to-lead-him-to-marylands-governorship-A7Y2XOG52VDPBFQSOWCS73J5VU/ |access-date=June 29, 2022}}</ref> After his win in the Republican gubernatorial primary, Cox denied organizing buses for the rally.<ref name="Gab">{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Md. GOP nominee Cox deletes account on Gab, site known for hate speech |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/08/24/gab-dan-cox-maryland-governor/ |access-date=September 28, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=August 24, 2022 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220824133320/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/08/24/gab-dan-cox-maryland-governor/ |archive-date=August 24, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Dacey |first1=Kim |title=Republican governor candidate Cox answers questions at Morgan State forum |url=https://www.wbaltv.com/article/republican-governor-candidate-cox-answers-questions-morgan-state-forum/41421528 |access-date=September 28, 2022 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=September 28, 2022}}</ref>

The Frederick County Democratic Central Committee began a letter-writing campaign calling for Cox to be expelled from the House of Delegates for his false claims.<ref name="gaskilljan8" /> Two days later Cox issued a statement denouncing "all mob violence including those who broke into the U.S. Capitol."<ref name="gaskilljan8">{{Cite news|url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/01/08/after-traitor-tweet-cox-denounces-mob-violence-at-u-s-capitol/|title=After 'Traitor' Tweet, Cox Denounces 'Mob Violence' at U.S. Capitol|first=Hannah|last=Gaskill|date=January 8, 2021|access-date=January 11, 2021|work=Maryland Matters}}</ref> In the statement Cox said he had attended the rally, but was not involved in the storming of the Capitol.<ref name="gaskilljan8" /> Governor [[Larry Hogan]] and Steven Clark, the chairman of the Frederick County Republican Central Committee, denounced Cox's comments, and delegate [[Kathleen Dumais]], the co-chair of the House Joint Committee on Legislative Ethics, said that the committee received some inquiries about Cox's tweets.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |title=Hogan Denounces Delegate's 'Traitor' Tweet, Says He Wants to Help 'Fix' GOP |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/01/12/hogan-denounces-delegates-traitor-tweet-says-he-wants-to-help-fix-gop/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=January 12, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Ryan |title=Fellow Republicans disavow Del. Cox's tweet calling VP Pence a traitor |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/fellow-republicans-disavow-del-coxs-tweet-calling-vp-pence-a-traitor/article_c5ee500c-98e7-5ad1-bf6e-974e4871aad8.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=January 7, 2021}}</ref>

In February 2022, representatives from [[Our Revolution]] and other progressive groups urged the [[Maryland State Board of Elections]] to consider blocking Cox from the ballot for his participation in the Capitol attack, citing [[Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution#Section 3: Disqualification from office for insurrection or rebellion|Section 3 of the 14th Amendment]] to the [[United States Constitution]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Citing Jan. 6 Actions, Progressive Groups Urge Elections Board Inquiry of Dan Cox |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/02/15/citing-jan-6-actions-progressive-groups-urge-elections-board-inquiry-of-dan-cox/ |access-date=February 15, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 15, 2022}}</ref> In May 2022, a lawsuit was filed against Maryland Elections Administrator [[Linda Lamone]], seeking to remove Cox from the [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election|2022 Republican primary ballot]] for his presence during the Capitol attack.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Kurtz |first2=Josh |last3=Shwe |first3=Elizabeth |title=Political Notes: Brown Poll Shows Big Lead, New Spots for Schulz and Baron, and More |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/05/17/political-notes-brown-poll-shows-big-lead-new-spots-for-schulz-and-baron-and-more/ |access-date=May 17, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 17, 2022}}</ref> Anne Arundel County [[circuit court]] judge Mark W. Crooks dismissed the case on May 20, 2022.<ref>{{cite web |title=State Board of Elections – June 2, 2022 Meeting |url=https://www.elections.maryland.gov/about/minutes/June_02_2022.pdf |website=elections.maryland.gov |publisher=Maryland State Board of Elections |access-date=July 25, 2022 |date=June 2, 2022}}</ref>

During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, when asked if he had any regrets for his actions during the 2020 presidential election and January 6 U.S. Capitol attack, Cox said that he regretted tweeting.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Dan Cox wants to talk about Maryland but can't escape Trump questions |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/dan-cox-governor-donald-trump-questions-UFPTJYUFY5BBPLV2DXMEDFO5FY/ |access-date=October 19, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=October 19, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

===2022 Maryland gubernatorial campaign===
{{see also|2022 Maryland gubernatorial election}}
[[File:Lt. Gov Tawes Crab and Clam Bake - 51588707850.jpg|thumb|right|Cox and Schifanelli at the [[Crisfield, Maryland#Annual events|J. Millard Tawes Crab and Clam Bake]] with Lieutenant Governor [[Boyd Rutherford]] in October 2021.]]
In late June 2021, Cox filed paperwork to [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election|run for governor in 2022]], and formally announced his candidacy on July 4, 2021.<ref>{{cite news |first=Mike |last=Sunnucks |url=https://www.stardem.com/news/state_news/trump-backer-del-dan-cox-files-for-potential-run-for-maryland-governor/article_0769cafa-a6df-517c-8b8f-424fe9bb78e1.html |title=Trump backer Del. Dan Cox files for potential run for Maryland governor |date=June 29, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Star Democrat]] |location=Easton, Maryland |access-date=June 30, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Republican Del. Dan Cox announces bid for Maryland governor in 2022, is first pro-Trump candidate in race |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2021/07/04/republican-del-dan-cox-announces-bid-for-maryland-governor-in-2022-is-first-pro-trump-candidate-in-race/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 4, 2021}}</ref> He launched his campaign with a campaign rally in [[Cambridge, Maryland|Cambridge]] on August 6, 2021.<ref>{{cite news |first=Hannah |last=Gaskill |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/08/06/cox-continues-to-shut-out-the-press-as-he-kicks-off-his-gubernatorial-bid/ |title=Cox Continues to Shut Out the Press as He Kicks Off His Gubernatorial Bid |date=August 6, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |access-date=August 9, 2021}}</ref> He picked Gordana Schifanelli, an [[Eastern Shore of Maryland|Eastern Shore]] lawyer, as his running mate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Del. Dan Cox picks Queen Anne's County lawyer as running mate in his bid for governor |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2021/09/29/del-dan-cox-picks-queen-annes-county-lawyer-as-running-mate-in-his-bid-for-governor/ |access-date=November 20, 2021 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 29, 2021}}</ref> On November 22, 2021, Cox received the endorsement of former president Donald Trump.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stole |first1=Bryn |title=Trump endorses Del. Dan Cox for Maryland governor while criticizing Hogan |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2021/11/23/trump-endorses-del-dan-cox-for-maryland-governor-while-criticizing-hogan/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=November 22, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | last1=Choi | first1=Joseph | title=Trump endorses challenger to Hogan ally in Maryland governor's race | url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/582727-trump-endorses-challenger-to-hogan-ally-in-maryland-governors-race | date=November 22, 2021 | work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] | access-date=November 22, 2021}}</ref>

Ahead of the primary election, Cox threatened lawsuits seeking to invalidate mail-in ballots.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid |title=Unpredictable Maryland Governor's Race Pits Old Guard vs. Upstarts |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/16/us/politics/maryland-primary-tom-perez-wes-moore.html |access-date=July 16, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=July 16, 2022}}</ref> Some political observers said before the primary that Cox would have publicly doubted the results if he had lost to [[Kelly M. Schulz|Kelly Schulz]].<ref name="OtherWesMoore">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |title=Maryland GOP nominee Dan Cox knocks Democratic gubernatorial foe Wes Moore's book in wake of lower polling, party disunity |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/19/maryland-gop-nominee-dan-cox-knocks-democratic-gubernatorial-foe-wes-moores-book-in-wake-of-lower-polling-party-disunity/ |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 19, 2022}}</ref>

{{external media
| float = right
| width = 258px
| video1 = [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oCcHQ8ZgC1w DGA Action Ad - Meet Dan]<br />via YouTube<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oCcHQ8ZgC1w |title=DGA Action Ad - Meet Dan |date=July 15, 2022 |access-date=September 21, 2022 |via=YouTube}}</ref>
}}

As polls showed Cox and Schulz running neck-and-neck in polls, the [[Democratic Governors Association]] spent $1 million for a television advertisement promoting Cox, hoping he would win the nomination and be easier for Democrats to defeat in November.<ref name="DGA Cox">{{cite news |last1=Tabb |first1=Michael |date=July 15, 2022 |title=Democrats Spent $1 Million On An Ad For A Far-Right Candidate |work=[[FiveThirtyEight]] |url=https://fivethirtyeight.com/videos/wait-who-are-kelly-schulz-and-dan-cox/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220715100618/https://fivethirtyeight.com/videos/wait-who-are-kelly-schulz-and-dan-cox/ |archive-date=July 15, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McCarty |first1=Dario |title=Democrats spend millions on Republican primaries |url=https://www.opensecrets.org/news/2022/07/democrats-spend-millions-on-republican-primaries/ |website=[[OpenSecrets]] |date=July 15, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Shapiro |first1=Ari |title=Why Democrats are paying for ads supporting Republican primary candidates |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/06/20/1106256047/why-democrats-are-paying-for-ads-supporting-republican-primary-candidates?t=1658336190311 |publisher=National Public Radio |date=June 20, 2022}}</ref> Cox denied receiving any support from the DGA, saying that he had "nothing to do with the ad purchase".<ref name="SchulzPush">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |title=Kelly Schulz campaign rails against national Democratic money being used to boost her GOP primary opponent |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/06/30/kelly-schulz-campaign-rails-against-national-democratic-money-being-used-to-boost-her-gop-primary-opponent/ |access-date=July 17, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=June 30, 2022}}</ref> Some observers, including strategist Jim Dornan, say that two factors—Trump's endorsement and the DGA ad blitz—allowed Cox to advance to the general election. Other observers, including former [[Lieutenant Governor of Maryland|Maryland lieutenant governor]] and [[Republican National Committee]] chair [[Michael Steele]], say the ads had little impact on voters, highlighting that far-right politician and [[neo-Confederate]] activist [[Michael Peroutka]] had won the [[2022 Maryland Attorney General election#Republican primary|Attorney General primary]] on the same ballot by an almost identical margin to Cox, even though the DGA did not run any ads on his behalf.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=As non-Trump Republicans bemoan party's shift, Dems move quickly to define Dan Cox |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/07/20/as-non-trump-republicans-bemoan-partys-shift-dems-move-quickly-to-define-dan-cox/ |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=July 20, 2022}}</ref>

Cox won the Republican primary on July 19, 2022, defeating Schulz with 52.0 percent of the vote.<ref name="GovernorVictory" /> If elected, he would have been the first governor from Frederick County since [[Enoch Louis Lowe]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Jack |title=Schulz or Cox could be first Frederick County governor since pre-Civil War |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_coverage/schulz-or-cox-could-be-first-frederick-county-governor-since-pre-civil-war/article_b0e00239-8712-5df2-9a42-07e49f447e1d.html |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=July 8, 2022}}</ref> At his victory party, Cox took photos with<ref>{{cite news |last1=Torres |first1=Matthew |title=Dan Cox captured taking picture with Maryland Proud Boys |url=https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/local/maryland/dan-cox-seen-taking-photo-with-maryland-proud-boys-per-wusa9-footage/65-09654b3d-0b60-4271-902d-3a9523c03ce5 |access-date=October 22, 2022 |work=[[WUSA (TV)]] |date=October 22, 2022}}</ref> and accepted a gift from a young man who introduced himself as a member of the [[Proud Boys|Maryland Proud Boys]]. The footage of this encounter, which was uploaded to Cox's [[Vimeo]] account, was deleted after ''[[The Washington Post]]'' contacted the Cox campaign, which responded with a statement denying an association with the young man.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Video shows GOP gov. nominee Dan Cox accepting gift from Md. Proud Boys |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/10/21/dan-cox-maryland-proud-boys/ |access-date=October 21, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=October 21, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Video shows Maryland gubernatorial nominee Dan Cox accepting a gift from a member of the Proud Boys |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/video-shows-maryland-gubernatorial-nominee-dan-cox-accepting-a-gift-from-a-member-of-the-proud-boys-YUVGZMBR7FG4VHAEJP3MLW3DMU/ |access-date=October 21, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=October 21, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Vigdor |first1=Neil |title=The Proud Boys Presented Dan Cox With a Gift. Now, He Says He Didn't Keep It. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/10/21/us/politics/proud-boys-dan-cox-md.html |access-date=October 22, 2022 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=October 21, 2022}}</ref>

Following his primary win, Cox sought to distance himself from the January 6 insurrection and former president Donald Trump, removing references to his endorsement from his website and making adjustments to his biography and issues pages. He also deactivated his account on [[Gab (social network)|Gab]], a website that has been described as a social media haven for white supremacists and neo-Nazis and was used by the perpetrator of the [[Pittsburgh synagogue shooting]], on which he had more than 1,000 posts.<ref name="Gab" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Gans |first1=Jared |title=Maryland GOP governor hopeful Dan Cox deletes account from site known for hate speech, Gab |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3613895-maryland-gop-governor-hopeful-dan-cox-deletes-account-from-site-known-for-hate-speech-gab/ |access-date=September 28, 2022 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=August 24, 2022}}</ref> Republican leaders expressed concern that Cox's primary victory would hurt their candidates downballot,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Jack |title=Some Republicans worry about Cox's effect on down-ballot races in November |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_coverage/some-republicans-worry-about-coxs-effect-on-down-ballot-races-in-november/article_4feaf2ca-614b-575e-b33e-2c83041e7e44.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=July 22, 2022}}</ref> with Senate minority leader [[Bryan Simonaire]] refusing to endorse or campaign with Cox<ref name="Simonaire">{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Maryland Senate GOP leader declines to endorse gubernatorial nominee Dan Cox during election push |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/06/maryland-senate-gop-leader-declines-to-endorse-gubernatorial-nominee-dan-cox-during-election-push/ |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 6, 2022}}</ref> and House minority leader [[Jason C. Buckel]] saying that Cox would need to moderate his views for the party to make gains.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dance |first1=Scott |last2=Jansech |first2=Sam |title=With far-right Dan Cox at top of ticket, choices for Maryland Republican leaders are fraught: vow support, push back or stay quiet? |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/07/24/with-far-right-dan-cox-at-top-of-ticket-choices-for-maryland-republican-leaders-are-fraught-vow-support-push-back-or-stay-quiet/ |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 24, 2022}}</ref> The Maryland Republican Party would end up losing two seats in the state Senate and three seats in the House of Delegates in the [[2022 Maryland elections#State legislature|2022 elections]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=Democrats retain legislative majorities, but some seats have shuffled between parties |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/11/09/democrats-retain-legislative-majorities-but-some-seats-have-shuffled-between-parties/ |access-date=April 28, 2024 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref>

Cox was defeated by [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee [[Wes Moore]] in the general election on November 8, 2022.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Democrat Wes Moore declares victory, will become Maryland's first Black governor |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/11/08/democrat-wes-moore-declares-victory-will-become-marylands-first-black-governor/ |access-date=November 9, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref> He initially declined to concede after the election was called for Moore by various national news outlets, believing that there was still a path to victory,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Baye |first1=Rachel |title=Maryland GOP nominee for governor Dan Cox not ready to concede |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2022-11-09/maryland-gop-nominee-for-governor-dan-cox-not-ready-to-concede |access-date=November 9, 2022 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref> but called Moore the next day to concede the election.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wes Moore wins Gubernatorial race, becomes Maryland's first Black governor |url=https://www.wmar2news.com/news/local-news/wes-moore-wins-gubernatorial-race-becomes-marylands-first-black-governor |access-date=November 9, 2022 |work=[[WMAR-TV]] |date=November 9, 2022}}</ref>

Following Cox's defeat, his running mate Gordana Schifanelli filed to run for Chair of the [[Maryland Republican Party]], seeking to succeed retiring chairman Dirk Haire, but was blocked from running because she filed an hour after the candidacy deadline.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Schifanelli bows out of GOP chair race after filing documents missed deadline by an hour |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/11/22/schifanelli-bows-out-of-gop-chair-race-after-filing-documents-missed-deadline-by-an-hour/ |access-date=November 29, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=November 22, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Barker |first1=Jeff |title=Former Maryland Lt. Gov. candidate Gordana Schifanelli's bid for GOP state chair is rebuffed in flare-up over filing deadline |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/11/23/former-maryland-lt-gov-candidate-gordana-schifanellis-bid-for-gop-state-chair-is-rebuffed-in-flare-up-over-filing-deadline/ |access-date=November 29, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=November 23, 2022}}</ref> Schifanelli later said that Cox blamed her for their loss and that the running mates rarely spoke to each other during the campaign, with their relationship souring well before the general election. Cox disputed this claim, telling ''The Washington Post'', "I never blamed Ms. Schifanelli for our election loss. Her comments are false and sadly self-serving."<ref name="MDGOP Chair">{{cite news |last1=Jamison |first1=Peter |title=Maryland GOP at war with itself after Dan Cox's failed governor's race |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/12/08/maryland-gop-dan-cox-schifanelli/ |access-date=December 9, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=December 9, 2022}}</ref> Cox later endorsed Nicole Beus Harris, a political consultant and the wife of U.S. Representative [[Andy Harris (politician)|Andy Harris]], as the next chair of the Maryland Republican Party.<ref name="MDGOP Chair" />

====Mail-in ballots lawsuit====
In August 2022, Cox said he opposed a lawsuit filed by the Maryland State Board of Elections to allow officials to count mail-in ballots ahead of Election Day, calling it unconstitutional.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Dan Cox says he will oppose step to count mail-in ballots earlier |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/08/20/dan-cox-says-he-will-oppose-step-to-count-mail-in-ballots-earlier/ |access-date=August 22, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=August 20, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Engel |first1=Amanda |title=Republican nominee for governor argues petition for earlier vote canvas is unconstitutional |url=https://www.wmar2news.com/news/election-2020/republican-nominee-for-governor-argues-petition-for-earlier-vote-canvas-is-unconstitutional |access-date=August 22, 2022 |work=[[WMAR-TV]] |date=August 19, 2022}}</ref> According to the [[National Conference of State Legislatures]], Maryland is the only state that restricts the processing of absentee ballots until after Election Day.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Koslof |first1=Evan |title=Maryland Republican Governor candidate Dan Cox and Board of Elections locked in legal battle over mail-in ballots |url=https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/verify/maryland-republican-governor-candidate-board-election-locked-legal-battle-over-mail-in-ballots-dan-cox/65-890bfc54-f322-46ea-9077-75955893696f |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[WUSA9]] |date=September 16, 2022}}</ref>

In September 2022 court papers and a hearing, Cox argued that the board lacked the authority to begin tabulating ballots before election day.<ref>{{cite news |first=Leonard N. |last=Fleming |title=Cox files court response opposing early mail-in ballot counting, says Maryland failed to prove true emergency |url=https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/local-news/maryland/exclusive-cox-filed-court-response-opposing-early-mail-in-ballot-counting-says-state-failed-to-prove-true-emergency/ |access-date=September 15, 2022 |work=[[WDCW]] |date=September 15, 2022 |archive-date=September 15, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220915150042/https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/local-news/maryland/exclusive-cox-filed-court-response-opposing-early-mail-in-ballot-counting-says-state-failed-to-prove-true-emergency/}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ryan |first1=Kate |title=Cox wants to block early counting of Maryland mail-in ballots |url=https://wtop.com/maryland/2022/09/cox-wants-to-block-early-counting-of-maryland-mail-in-ballots/ |access-date=September 16, 2022 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=September 16, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Elwood |first1=Karina |title=Dan Cox questions election integrity as Md. officials ask judge for help |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/20/maryland-mail-vote-counting-lawsuit/ |access-date=September 20, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=September 20, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Montgomery judge weighing Board of Elections request to speed up mail-in ballot processing in November |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/09/20/montgomery-judge-weighing-board-of-elections-request-to-speed-up-mail-in-ballot-processing-in-november/ |access-date=September 20, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=September 20, 2022}}</ref> On September 23, 2022, a Montgomery County Circuit Court judge ruled in favor of the board, allowing it to begin counting mail-in ballots on October 1.<ref>{{cite news |title=Judge rules Maryland boards of elections can count mail-in ballots before Election Day |url=https://www.wbaltv.com/article/maryland-board-of-elections-can-count-mail-in-ballots-early/41351893 |access-date=September 23, 2022 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=September 23, 2022}}</ref> Cox appealed to the [[Appellate Court of Maryland]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Opilo |first1=Emily |title=Republican gubernatorial candidate Dan Cox to appeal order allowing October ballot counting |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/27/republican-gubernatorial-candidate-dan-cox-to-appeal-order-allowing-october-ballot-counting-in-maryland/ |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 27, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Elwood |first1=Karina |title=Dan Cox appeals Md. decision to allow early counting of mail-in ballots |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/09/27/cox-appeals-maryland-early-counting/ |access-date=September 27, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=September 27, 2022}}</ref> which denied his request to halt the counting of mail-in ballots.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCord |first1=Joel |title=Dan Cox's appeal to delay early mail-in ballot count denied by judge |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2022-09-29/dan-coxs-appeal-to-delay-early-mail-in-ballot-count-denied-by-judge |access-date=October 4, 2022 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=September 29, 2022}}</ref> In October 7, 2022, [[Supreme Court of Maryland|Maryland's highest court]] unanimously<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lash |first1=Steve |title=Md. high court explains unanimous rejection of Cox's challenge to early vote counting |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2023/04/06/md-high-court-unanimously-rejected-coxs-challenge-to-early-vote-counting/ |access-date=April 7, 2023 |work=[[The Daily Record (Maryland)|The Daily Record]] |date=April 6, 2023}}</ref> rejected a further appeal from Cox.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Maryland's highest court allows early counting of mail ballots in general election |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/marylands-highest-court-hearing-mail-ballot-counting-case-friday-AQ5OVIVNYRBYHKIKBO4X5DZT2Y/ |access-date=October 7, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=October 7, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Engel |first1=Amanda |title=Maryland Court of Appeals upholds lower court ruling on mail-in ballots after hearing arguments |url=https://www.wmar2news.com/news/election-2020/maryland-court-of-appeals-hears-arguments-on-early-counting-of-mail-in-ballots |access-date=October 7, 2022 |work=[[WMAR]] |date=October 7, 2022}}</ref> In an opinion written by Chief Justice [[Matthew J. Fader]] in March 2023, the court held that a section of the state's Election Law Article referenced in his lawsuit did not "violate the [[separation of powers]] guaranteed in the [[Maryland Declaration of Rights|Maryland Constitution's Declaration of Rights]]".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Segelbaum |first1=Dylan |last2=Sullivan |first2=Emily |last3=Wood |first3=Pamela |title=Banner political notes: It's poll season; FBI HQ in the budget; First family's first pitch; Bills are moving; The final word on Dan Cox's legal fight |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/local-government/banner-political-notes-its-poll-season-fbi-hq-in-the-budget-first-familys-first-pitch-bills-are-moving-the-final-word-on-dan-coxs-legal-fight-UXP624PAVFFGTJLOGG6BVHPWG4/ |access-date=April 1, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=April 1, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Elwood |first1=Karina |title=Md. appeals court disagrees with Cox, allows early mail-in vote counting |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/10/07/maryland-early-counting-court-appeal/ |access-date=October 7, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=October 7, 2022}}</ref> In February 2023, the [[U.S. Supreme Court]] declined to hear Cox's challenge, letting the ruling stand without comment.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lash |first1=Steve |title=U.S. Supreme Court declines Cox's appeal of early counting in 2022 election |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2023/02/21/u-s-supreme-court-declines-coxs-appeal-of-early-counting-in-2022-election/ |access-date=February 21, 2023 |work=The Daily Record |date=February 21, 2023}}</ref>

Cox did not commit to accepting the results of the election with mail-in ballots being counted early.<ref name="OtherWesMoore" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Baye |first1=Rachel |title=Cox won't say if he will accept election outcome |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2022-09-20/cox-wont-say-if-he-will-accept-election-outcome |access-date=September 20, 2022 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=September 20, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Down 22 points in a new poll, Dan Cox insists he has a path to victory |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/09/19/down-22-points-in-a-new-poll-dan-cox-insists-he-has-a-path-to-victory/ |access-date=September 19, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=September 19, 2022}}</ref> During a debate on October 12, Cox was non-committal when asked if he would accept the results of the election: "I have always accepted the election results that are fair and that are following the Constitution. At this point, it would be similar to saying that before a surgery takes place to decide whether or not the surgery went well".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |title=Five takeaways from what's clearly the only debate between Maryland gubernatorial candidates Wes Moore and Dan Cox |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/10/13/five-takeaways-from-whats-clearly-the-only-debate-between-maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-wes-moore-and-dan-cox/ |access-date=October 13, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=October 13, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Moore, Cox face off over credibility in Maryland debate |url=https://www.wbal.com/article/587273/2/moore-cox-face-off-over-credibility-in-maryland-debate |access-date=October 13, 2022 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=October 12, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Touchberry |first1=Ramsey |title=Maryland gubernatorial candidates trade barbs in lone debate to decide Larry Hogan successor |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2022/oct/12/dan-cox-wes-moore-trade-barbs-maryland-governors-d/ |access-date=October 13, 2022 |work=[[The Washington Times]] |date=October 12, 2022}}</ref> Ahead of the election, Cox called on his supporters to "monitor" the state's election drop boxes, alleging without evidence that the drop boxes were "regularly misused and stuffed with nefarious ballots".<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |last2=Kurtz |first2=Josh |title=Report from day 1 of early voting: 'Nobody's being really unpleasant because everyone's entitled to their opinion' |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/10/27/report-from-day-1-of-early-voting-nobodys-being-really-unpleasant-because-everyones-entitled-to-their-opinion/ |access-date=October 31, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=October 27, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Duffy |first1=Ken |title=Republican gubernatorial candidate Dan Cox wants volunteers to 'monitor' ballot boxes |url=https://www.wbal.com/article/589692/3/republican-gubernatorial-candidate-dan-cox-wants-volunteers-to-monitor-ballot-drop-boxes |access-date=October 31, 2022 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=October 29, 2022}}</ref>

===Post-legislative career===
After being defeated in the 2022 Maryland gubernatorial election, Cox returned to private practice in [[Frederick County, Maryland]]. In February 2023, [[Pennsylvania State Senate|Pennsylvania state senator]] [[Doug Mastriano]] hired Cox as his chief of staff. Cox also started a podcast titled ''It's Your Freedom!'', with the first episode premiering on [[Facebook]] on February 7.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Former GOP gubernatorial candidate Dan Cox joins Doug Mastriano's staff in Pennsylvania |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/dan-cox-doug-mastriano-MCWRNV4FJBA6BARGK56H7SUTEM/ |access-date=February 9, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=February 9, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Dan Cox joins Pennsylvania Sen. Doug Mastriano as chief of staff |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2023/02/09/former-maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-dan-cox-joins-pennsylvania-sen-doug-mastriano-as-chief-of-staff/ |access-date=February 9, 2023 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=February 9, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ford |first1=William J. |last2=Gaines |first2=Danielle E. |last3=Zorzi |first3=William F. |title=Political Notes: Prince George's House nominee would make history, Dan Cox's new gig, keeping up with Speaker Jones |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/02/10/political-notes-prince-georges-house-nominee-would-make-history-dan-coxs-new-gig-keeping-up-with-speaker-jones/ |access-date=February 10, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 10, 2023}}</ref> Cox resigned as Mastriano's chief of staff in November 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Christopher |first1=Tirzah |title=Pa. Senate quietly spent at least $133K settling discrimination complaint against Mastriano |url=https://www.pennlive.com/politics/2025/11/pa-senate-quietly-spent-at-least-133k-settling-discrimination-complaint-against-mastriano.html |access-date=January 12, 2026 |work=[[The Patriot-News]] |date=November 21, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

Also in February 2023, [[Wicomico County, Maryland|Wicomico County]] executive Julie Giordano nominated Cox to serve as special counsel for the county. Cox was scheduled to join the county council meeting to discuss any questions and concerns councilmembers had about his nomination, but was not present during the session. The Wicomico County Council voted 6–1 to reject Cox's nomination.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ford |first1=William J. |last2=Sears |first2=Bryan |title=Political Notes: Davis re-elected as state treasurer, Cox loses another vote, Schiraldi gets confirmed |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/02/21/political-notes-davis-re-elected-as-state-treasurer-cox-loses-another-vote-schiraldi-gets-confirmed/ |access-date=February 21, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 21, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaime |first1=Kristian |title=Dan Cox rejected as special counsel as Wicomico County Council session gets heated |url=https://www.delmarvanow.com/story/news/local/maryland/2023/02/21/dan-cox-rejected-as-wicomico-county-special-counsel-amid-public-outcry/69928317007/ |access-date=February 21, 2023 |work=[[The Daily Times (Salisbury, Maryland)|The Daily Times]] |location=[[Salisbury, Maryland]] |date=February 21, 2023}}</ref>

In April 2023, Cox was hired by Robert Krop, a local firearms business owner who alongside Frederick County Sheriff [[Chuck Jenkins]] was charged by federal prosecutors with conspiracy and making false statements to acquire machine guns, to represent him in his federal trial.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Niel |first1=Clara |title=Frederick firearm business owner pleads not guilty to federal firearm charges |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/courts/frederick-firearm-business-owner-pleads-not-guilty-to-federal-firearm-charges/article_98278c46-0d05-5546-bd3e-55eab44c1be7.html |access-date=April 13, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=April 13, 2023}}</ref> In June 2023, Judge [[Stephanie A. Gallagher]] denied a motion to dismiss the charges against Krop,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Atelsek |first1=Jillian |title=Judge rebukes Krop, denies majority of pretrial motions in firearms case |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/cops_and_crime/judge-rebukes-krop-denies-majority-of-pretrial-motions-in-firearms-case/article_4eb8ffbb-39d3-5b7f-ac97-2470fb5ec5fb.html |access-date=June 28, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=June 27, 2023}}</ref> in which Cox alleged that the indictment was "politically motivated" and questioned the Ukrainian citizenship of the lead [[ATF]] agent in the case.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fenton |first1=Justin |title=Dan Cox calls prosecution of Frederick Co. sheriff, gun dealer a 'political scheme' |url=https://www.thebaltimorebanner.com/community/criminal-justice/frederick-county-sheriff-charles-jenkins-responds-indictment-AD2JBORUXRDLLNOSN3YNRMTXUY/ |access-date=June 21, 2023 |work=[[Baltimore Banner]] |date=May 31, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Augenstein |first1=Neal |title=Gun dealer indicted with Frederick Co. sheriff claims 'political vindictiveness' |url=https://wtop.com/frederick-county/2023/06/gun-dealer-indicted-with-frederick-co-sheriff-seeks-to-have-case-dropped-claims-political-vindictiveness/ |access-date=June 21, 2023 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=June 6, 2023}}</ref> Jenkins' attorneys also criticized Cox's motion, saying that he was employing "inconsistent, even hostile, defense strategies" and asking to be tried separately from Krop,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Atelsek |first1=Jillian |title=Jenkins implores judge to be tried separately from Krop, calls Cox's legal strategy "dangerous" |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/cops_and_crime/jenkins-implores-judge-to-be-tried-separately-from-krop-calls-coxs-legal-strategy-dangerous/article_80c014d2-1408-501e-9587-d57070727b63.html |access-date=July 19, 2023 |work=[[The Frederick News-Post]] |date=June 30, 2023}}</ref> which was granted on August 31, 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Niel |first1=Clara |title=Judge orders separate trials for Jenkins, Krop |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/courts/judge-orders-separate-trials-for-jenkins-krop/article_5e8d1b50-1da6-5ecf-a216-4b4eaa700f78.html |access-date=September 8, 2023 |work=[[The Frederick News-Post]] |date=August 31, 2023}}</ref> Gallagher dismissed the federal indictment against Krop in May 2024, stating that his [[right to a speedy trial]] was violated;<ref>{{cite news |last1=Niel |first1=Clara |title=Federal judge dismisses indictment against business owner in machine gun conspiracy case |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/machine_gun_trial/federal-judge-dismisses-indictment-against-business-owner-in-machine-gun-conspiracy-case/article_8737efe8-c6b6-562b-86be-3e025bf1ec41.html |access-date=May 3, 2024 |work=[[The Frederick News-Post]] |date=May 3, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> federal prosecutors filed new indictments against Krop later that month.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jacoby |first1=Ceoli |title=Federal prosecutors file new indictment against Krop on machine gun charges |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/machine_gun_trial/federal-prosecutors-file-new-indictment-against-krop-on-machine-gun-charges/article_0e6a2deb-1280-5e58-8adc-ac9022bff3a5.html |access-date=June 3, 2024 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=May 31, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> Krop was acquitted on all counts in October 2024.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jacoby |first1=Ceoli |title=Updated: Robert Krop, owner of The Machine Gun Nest, acquitted on all counts |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/machine_gun_trial/updated-robert-krop-owner-of-the-machine-gun-nest-acquitted-on-all-counts/article_e087f28e-1c6a-5a12-99d7-92c3869ebad9.html |access-date=October 24, 2024 |work=The Frederick News-Post |date=October 22, 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

In May 2024, following the jury's guilty verdict in the [[Trump hush money trial]], Cox called on the [[Maryland Republican Party]] to censure former governor [[Larry Hogan]] for calling on elected officials to not "pour fuel on the fire with more toxic partisanship" amid the ruling.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Schwartzman |first2=Paul |title=Hogan provokes ire within Trumpworld: 'You just ended your campaign' |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/05/31/hogan-comment-ire-trump/ |access-date=June 1, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=May 31, 2024}}</ref> In November 2024, after Trump won the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]], Cox told ''[[The Daily Record (Maryland)|The Daily Record]]'' that he was being considered for [[U.S. Attorney for the District of Maryland]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Neill |first1=Madeleine |title=Veteran Baltimore lawyer vies for MD U.S. attorney job with Trump loyalist |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2024/11/14/veteran-baltimore-lawyer-vies-for-md-u-s-attorney-job-with-trump-loyalist/ |access-date=November 14, 2024 |work=The Daily Record |date=November 14, 2024}}</ref> If appointed to the seat and confirmed by the [[U.S. Senate]], Cox told ''[[The Baltimore Sun]]'' that his priorities would include prosecuting sex trafficking and criminal gangs, such as [[MS-13]], and ensuring Maryland elections are transparent.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Conrad |first1=Brooke |title=Who will Trump pick as Maryland's top federal prosecutor? Former Catherine Pugh attorney and a Republican delegate are vying for the spot |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2024/11/19/pugh-attorney-and-former-republican-delegate-vying-for-trumps-pick-as-marylands-top-federal-prosecutor/ |access-date=November 19, 2024 |work=The Baltimore Sun |date=November 19, 2024}}</ref>

===2024 congressional campaign===
{{main|2024 Maryland's 6th congressional district election}}
In May 2023, Cox said he was considering a run for Congress in [[Maryland's 6th congressional district]] in [[2024 United States House of Representatives elections in Maryland#District 6|2024]], seeking to succeed outgoing U.S. Representative [[David Trone]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |last2=Sears |first2=Bryan P. |title=Risks and opportunities for both parties as race to replace Trone ramps up |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/05/10/risks-and-opportunities-for-both-parties-as-race-to-replace-trone-ramps-up/ |access-date=July 18, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 10, 2023}}</ref> On July 3, 2023, someone filed paperwork with the [[Federal Election Commission]] to enter Cox into the 2024 election, which prompted him to report the filing to the FEC for fraud.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sforza |first1=Lauren |last2=Shapero |first2=Julia |title=Dan Cox says he has not filed to run for House, has reported FEC filing for fraud |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/4079540-dan-cox-says-he-has-not-filed-to-run-for-house/ |access-date=July 18, 2023 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=July 3, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=Someone files papers for Dan Cox to run for Congress — but he says it wasn't him |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/07/03/someone-files-papers-for-dan-cox-to-run-for-congress-but-he-says-it-wasnt-him/ |access-date=July 18, 2023 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=July 3, 2023}}</ref> According to a ''[[Daily Beast]]'' investigation, it was Cox's campaign treasurer, Tom Datwyler, who filed the FEC paperwork on his behalf after creating fundraising ads on [[WinRed]] for Cox's congressional campaign. Datwyler was told by Cox advisor Rory McShane to terminate the FEC filing the next day, saying that Cox had decided against running in 2024.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sollenberger |first1=Roger |title=GOP Candidate Reported His Own False Filing to the Feds |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/gop-candidate-dan-cox-reported-his-own-false-filing-to-the-feds |access-date=July 18, 2023 |work=[[The Daily Beast]] |date=July 17, 2023}}</ref>

Despite these emails, Cox announced that he would run for Congress on October 30, 2023.<ref name="Cox2024">{{cite news |last1=Bixby |first1=Ginny |title=Dan Cox announces run for Maryland Sixth Congressional District |url=https://moco360.media/2023/10/30/dan-cox-announces-run-for-maryland-sixth-congressional-district/ |access-date=October 30, 2023 |work=[[MoCo360]] |date=October 30, 2023}}</ref> Cox was seen as a frontrunner, alongside former state delegate [[Neil Parrott]], in the Republican primary,<ref name="FlipMD06" /> during which he sought to associate himself with former president [[Donald Trump]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |last2=Gaines |first2=Danielle E. |title=Political notes: Lobby firm partner leaving firm he helped start, Cox touts Trump poll, Cardin addresses controversy |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2023/12/20/political-notes-lobby-firm-partner-leaving-firm-he-helped-start-cox-touts-trump-poll-cardin-addresses-controversy/ |access-date=February 13, 2024 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=December 20, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jacoby |first1=Ceoli |title=With primary fields set in 6th District congressional race, candidates stake their positions |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_coverage/with-primary-fields-set-in-6th-district-congressional-race-candidates-stake-their-positions/article_2677bcbc-eefc-54e6-bb81-90f73eb766c9.html |access-date=February 13, 2024 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=February 12, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> calling the [[Indictments against Donald Trump|ongoing criminal cases]] against Trump a "witch hunt" and continuing to promote disproven conspiracy theories that the 2020 presidential election was marred by fraud,<ref name="FlipMD06">{{cite news |last1=Thompson |first1=Steve |title=Republicans in Maryland navigate Trump effect in run to flip House seat |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/04/28/maryland-sixth-district-republicans/ |access-date=April 28, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=April 27, 2024}}</ref> and ran on a platform including immigration, crime, and opposing [[COVID-19 pandemic]] health restrictions.<ref>{{cite news |title=More candidates provide information ahead of Feb. 17 Republican Forum |url=https://www.wvnews.com/garrettrepublican/news/more-candidates-provide-information-ahead-of-feb-17-republican-forum/article_89c6899a-c9d6-11ee-b5b7-733d82376864.html |access-date=February 19, 2024 |work=WV News |date=February 15, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> Cox struggled to build a coalition of voters without the outside support he received during his 2022 gubernatorial campaign<ref>{{cite news |last1=George |first1=Olivia |last2=Shepherd |first2=Katie |title=Dan Cox files to run for Md. governor, eyeing possible rematch with Moore |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2026/01/31/dan-cox-maryland-governor-bid-wes-moore/ |access-date=January 31, 2026 |work=[[The Washington Post]] |date=January 31, 2026}}</ref> and was defeated in the Republican primary election on May 14, 2024,<ref name="ParrottPrimary" /> placing second behind Parrott with 30.1 percent of the vote.<ref name="2024 Primary Results" />

===2026 Maryland gubernatorial election===
{{main|2026 Maryland gubernatorial election}}
In January 2026, Cox announced that he would again run for governor of Maryland in [[2026 Maryland gubernatorial election|2026]], challenging Democratic incumbent [[Wes Moore]]. His running mate is Robert Krop, a firearms business owner from [[Frederick County, Maryland|Frederick County]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marshall |first1=Ryan |title=Cox files to run for Md. governor, with Krop as running mate |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/elections/cox-files-to-run-for-md-governor-with-krop-as-running-mate/article_e93bc106-82b7-510c-bb06-cfd3232c506d.html |access-date=January 30, 2026 |work=[[The Frederick News-Post]] |date=January 30, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> During the Republican primary, he ran on a platform of tax cuts and increases to local government aid, housing affordability, and in-state energy generation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=9 GOP MD governor candidates a stark contrast to party in Larry Hogan's day |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2026/05/20/republican-governor-candidates-larry-hogan/ |access-date=May 20, 2026 |work=[[Daily Record (Maryland)|The Daily Record]] |date=May 20, 2026}}</ref>

Cox was viewed as a frontrunner in the Republican primary alongside businessman [[Ed Hale (businessman)|Ed Hale]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crawford |first1=Ian |title=Moore officially launches reelection bid with Baltimore, Prince George's events |url=https://marylandmatters.org/briefs/moore-officially-launches-reelection-bid-with-baltimore-prince-georges-events/ |access-date=May 4, 2026 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |publisher=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 3, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bazos |first1=Racquel |title=Wes Moore launches reelection campaign in Baltimore, Prince George's County |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/05/02/wes-moore-reelection-campaign/ |access-date=May 3, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=May 2, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=A party-switcher and a MAGA loyalist are the GOP's top options to take on Moore |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/maryland-republican-governor-cox-hale-AEH7YRQPWNHA7CRVY4LFVKOTXI/ |access-date=June 17, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=June 17, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> who Cox criticized for switching parties in summer 2025 to run for governor in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Governor rematch: Democrat Moore will face GOP's Cox again |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/maryland-governor-primary-election-2026-BBC7RVV2FBAMBBNS6UZR2I27WM/ |access-date=June 24, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=June 23, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> In June 2026, the [[Maryland Democratic Party]] and Governor Moore began sending out mailers to Republican primary voters and airing advertisements promoting Cox over Hale.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Epstein |first1=Reid J. |title=Democrats Keep Helping Out This Far-Right Maryland Republican |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/16/us/politics/wes-moore-dan-cox-maryland.html |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 16, 2026}}</ref> Cox and Hale condemned the spending, with Cox calling the Democrats' efforts "exactly what unpopular governors like Wes Moore do to distract from his disastrous policies".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=Democrats weigh in on the Republican primary for governor with TV ads and mailers |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/maryland-democratic-party-ads-republican-primary-dan-cox-ed-hale-HXSTDHAVIRBNXLGM4SLACQZDEI/ |access-date=June 16, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=June 16, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Cox won the Republican primary election on June 23, 2026, defeating Hale with 43.3 percent of the vote.<ref name="tops">{{cite news |last1=Hammann |first1=Will |last2=Sears |first2=Bryan P. |title=Cox tops Hale in GOP primary, heads to rematch with Moore for governor |url=https://marylandmatters.org/2026/06/23/moore-cruises-to-renomination-as-republicans-duke-it-out/ |access-date=June 24, 2026 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=June 23, 2026}}</ref>

During the general election, Cox says he will campaign on lowering taxes, restoring in-state energy production, and increasing parental involvement in public schools.<ref name="CoxAffordability">{{cite news |last1=Chingarande |first1=Tinashe |title=Dan Cox says Maryland affordability crisis gives GOP new opening in 2026 |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/07/08/dan-cox-says-maryland-affordability-crisis-gives-gop-new-opening-in-2026/ |access-date=July 8, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 8, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Chingarande |first1=Tinashe |title=Maryland GOP leaders: Cox must address voter concerns, Moore's record |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2026/06/25/maryland-gop-leaders-cox-must-center-voter-concerns-moores-broken-promises/ |access-date=June 25, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=June 25, 2026}}</ref> He is also expected to rally against Moore's handling of economic issues, the [[Francis Scott Key Bridge replacement]], and his military record.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sanderlin |first1=Lee O. |title=Gov. Wes Moore doesn't commit to full second term if reelected |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/moore-democrat-white-house-president-2028-trump-UTX3I6WSUVAVHOKYMA5S4VEJ74/ |access-date=October 6, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=October 6, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Cox also sought to distance himself from the positions he took during his 2022 gubernatorial campaign as well as President [[Donald Trump]] amid Democratic efforts to remind voters of Trump's endorsement of Cox in 2022, saying that he did not "have an association or affiliation with the President" and would not be seeking his endorsement in the 2026 election, and that [[Joe Biden]] "probably" won the [[2020 United States presidential election]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Republican nominee Dan Cox bites back at Gov. Moore on Maryland redistricting |url=https://wtop.com/maryland/2026/07/republican-nominee-dan-cox-bites-back-at-gov-moore-on-maryland-redistricting/ |access-date=July 1, 2026 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=July 1, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Wood |first1=Pamela |title=GOP governor candidate Cox seeks to distance himself from Trump's election denial |url=https://www.thebanner.com/politics-power/state-government/dan-cox-wes-moore-election-trump-S5ED527BMBFC5DPTDPJMY5VSGQ/ |access-date=July 16, 2026 |work=[[The Baltimore Banner]] |date=July 16, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

==Political views and statements==
===Abortion===
Cox identifies as [[pro-life]].<ref name="Cox2018" /><ref name="MSUforum" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sears |first1=Bryan |title=Moore, Cox clash on crime, abortion and fitness for office |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2022/10/12/moore-cox-clash-on-crime-abortion-and-election-fitness-for-office/ |access-date=October 13, 2022 |work=[[The Daily Record (Maryland)|The Daily Record]] |date=October 12, 2022}}</ref> In 2019, [[NARAL Pro-Choice America|NARAL Pro-Choice Maryland]], which supports abortion, gave Cox a 73% score. [[National Right to Life Committee|Maryland Right to Life]], which opposes abortion, gave Cox a 92% score during his first term in the Maryland House of Delegates.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dan Cox's Ratings and Endorsements |url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/evaluations/167340/dan-cox |website=VoteSmart.org |access-date=September 27, 2022}}</ref>

During the 2019 legislative session, Cox was one of fourteen state delegates to vote against the state budget, citing the defeat of an amendment blocking state funding for abortions.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=House passes budget and considers $1.5 million for downtown hotel |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/education/funding/house-passes-budget-and-considers-1-5-million-for-downtown-hotel/article_1f895949-0bb6-5687-a755-5d34a1f3f1c8.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=March 14, 2019}}</ref>

In February 2021, Cox joined delegates [[Kathy Szeliga]] and [[Sid Saab]] at a protest against abortion at the Maryland State House.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sanchez |first1=Olivia |title=Anti-abortion advocates gather for socially distanced rally outside State House in Annapolis |url=https://www.capitalgazette.com/politics/ac-cn-march-for-life-20210204-20210205-eaeoaekxznbujkktxfcictry5m-story.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[The Capital]] |date=February 4, 2021}}</ref> During his first term in the legislature, Cox introduced fourteen bills that would restrict abortion access and offered budget amendments to remove state funding for the procedure.<ref name="Backbench" /> He also cosponsored several bills that would prohibit abortions if a [[Six-week abortion ban|fetal heartbeat]] is detected,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marbella |first1=Jean |title=Post-Roe, continuing furor over abortion rights decision looms over November election in Maryland and beyond |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/09/post-roe-continuing-furor-over-abortion-rights-decision-looms-over-november-election-in-maryland-and-beyond/ |access-date=September 23, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 9, 2022}}</ref> including the Maryland Fetal Heartbeat Protection Act (HB 1195).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Arias |first1=Jeremy |title=Activists in Frederick join nationwide reproductive rights protest |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/activists-in-frederick-join-nationwide-reproductive-rights-protest/article_f6524d36-3dec-5b40-9912-ca393991519c.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=May 21, 2019}}</ref>

In June 2022, Cox praised the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]]'s ruling in ''[[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization]]'', which overturned ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' and ''[[Planned Parenthood v. Casey]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=Maryland After Roe Is Extinguished: 'It's Going to Be a Different World' |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/06/24/maryland-after-roe-is-extinguished-its-going-to-be-a-different-world/ |access-date=June 24, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=June 24, 2022}}</ref> During his 2022 gubernatorial campaign, Cox said that he would end taxpayer funding through [[Medicaid]] for abortions<ref name="Banner bio" /> and opposed using taxpayer funding to provide contraception options for college students or to women traveling from other states to obtain abortion services in Maryland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marquez |first1=Alexandra |title=Cox defends his conservative platform at HBCU forum |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/meetthepressblog/cox-defends-conservative-platform-hbcu-forum-rcna49720 |access-date=September 28, 2022 |work=[[NBC News]] |date=September 28, 2022}}</ref> Cox declined to say whether he would support federal restrictions on abortions, including a bill introduced by Senator [[Lindsey Graham]] that would ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy.<ref name="CoxWT" /> During a debate on October 12, 2022, Cox said that he supported exceptions for rape, incest, or for the life of the mother.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Frost |first1=Mikenzie |title=Maryland's gubernatorial candidates Wes Moore and Dan Cox clash in first and only debate |url=https://foxbaltimore.com/news/local/marylands-gubernatorial-candidates-wes-moore-and-dan-cox-clash-in-first-and-only-debate |access-date=October 13, 2022 |work=[[WBFF]] |date=October 12, 2022}}</ref>

===Climate change and the environment===
Cox [[climate change denial|rejects]] the [[scientific consensus on climate change]]<ref>{{cite news |last1=Turque |first1=Bill |title=In Maryland's 8th District, congressional candidates tack hard to left and right |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/in-marylands-district-8-congressional-candidates-tack-hard-to-left-and-right/2016/10/15/b527deb4-8fc7-11e6-9c85-ac42097b8cc0_story.html |access-date=November 27, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=October 15, 2016}}</ref> and opposes tax subsidies for [[clean energy]] programs.<ref name="Reaganomics" /> He voted against the Climate Solutions Now Act of 2021 (SB 414),<ref>{{cite web |title=Legislation - SB0414 |url=https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/SB0414?ys=2021RS |publisher=[[Maryland General Assembly]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Voting Report - 1354.pdf |url=https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/2021RS/votes/house/1354.pdf |publisher=[[Maryland General Assembly]] |access-date=November 27, 2021}}</ref> a proposed climate bill that would have included a goal to reduce [[greenhouse gas emissions]] to a level that is 50 percent lower than it was in 2006 by 2030.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shwe |first1=Elizabeth |title=Climate Bill Dies as House and Senate Fail to Compromise |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/04/13/climate-bill-dies-as-house-and-senate-fail-to-compromise/ |access-date=November 27, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=April 13, 2021}}</ref> He voted against the bill when it was re-introduced in the 2022 legislative session.<ref name="MooreCox Sun" />

During the 2019 legislative session, Cox said he opposed a bill to ban the pesticide [[chlorpyrifos]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Md. Senate to consider statewide ban of the pesticide chlorpyrifos |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/economy_and_business/agriculture/md-senate-to-consider-statewide-ban-of-the-pesticide-chlorpyrifos/article_6d72c120-565d-50fa-b63a-295e720c840b.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=March 29, 2019}}</ref>

During the 2020 legislative session, Cox supported legislation that would provide a [[sales tax]] exemption for [[data center]] equipment.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Moore, Cox records on MD data centers are mixed |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2026/09/10/data-centers-wes-moore-dan-cox-maryland/ |access-date=September 10, 2026 |work=The Daily Record |date=September 10, 2026}}</ref> During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, he supported imposing a new "data center electric tax" on [[data center]]s,<ref name="CoxAffordability" /> banning data center development in Maryland, and imposing a data center moratorium if elected governor.<ref name="WBAL2026" />

In September 2022, Cox said he would support repealing the state's ban on [[fracking]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Larry |first1=Greg |title='Freedom' at stake, says GOP nominee Cox |url=https://www.times-news.com/news/freedom-at-stake-says-gop-nominee-cox/article_0218d0e8-3085-11ed-8785-939bfa0cdc05.html |access-date=September 10, 2022 |work=[[Cumberland Times-News]] |date=September 10, 2022}}</ref>

In October 2022, Cox told ''[[Lancaster Farming]]'' that he supported efforts to clean up the [[Chesapeake Bay]]. Cox said he considered reducing effluent from bay area sewage systems and ending silt pouring from the [[Conowingo Dam]] to be a "top priority".<ref name="Lancaster" />

During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, Cox supported prioritizing funding for clean energy projects in Maryland, but opposed [[offshore wind farms]]<ref name="MeetEight" /> and the [[Maryland Piedmont Reliability Project]].<ref name="WBAL2026">{{cite news |last1=Levchuck |first1=Peter |title=Cox speaks of electricity crisis, economic issues in governor's race |url=https://www.wbaltv.com/article/dan-cox-maryland-governors-race-electricity-economy-interview/72737018 |access-date=July 16, 2026 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=July 16, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> He supported modernizing sewage and stormwater systems to protect the Chesapeake Bay, but hoped to do it in a way that "doesn't crush the farmers, and that also preserves our Bay".<ref name="Foreback" />

===COVID-19 pandemic===
In April 2020, Cox posted a portion of the [[Constitution of Maryland|state constitution]] on his Facebook page after [[Governor of Maryland|Maryland governor]] [[Larry Hogan]] issued an executive order implementing a stay-at-home directive.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Del. Dan Cox asking for opinions on stay-at-home order |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/coronavirus/del-dan-cox-asking-for-opinions-on-stay-at-home-order/article_8e28a715-3bc6-5cfa-8b4f-4ae1b8d9d5b2.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=April 2, 2020}}</ref> He later challenged the legality of Hogan's statewide [[mask mandate]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=GOP Lawmakers From Rural Areas Want Hogan to Ease Restrictions |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2020/04/16/gop-lawmakers-from-rural-areas-want-hogan-to-ease-restrictions/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=April 16, 2020}}</ref> Cox claimed in the court filing that he was threatened with arrest by Hogan's advisers if he attended and spoke at a rally protesting the stay-at-home orders; Hogan spokesperson rebutted with, "We fully respect Delegate Cox's right to protest and express his feelings, but that doesn't entitle him to make false and baseless claims."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/religious-leaders-business-owners-and-lawmakers-file-federal-lawsuit-over-hogans-stay-at-home-orders/2020/05/03/2d13143e-8d4e-11ea-a9c0-73b93422d691_story.html |title=Religious leaders, business owners and lawmakers file federal lawsuit over Hogan's stay-at-home orders |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=May 3, 2020 |first1=Ann E. |last1=Marimow |first2=Erin |last2=Cox |access-date=July 27, 2025}}</ref> [[U.S. District Court Judge]] [[Catherine C. Blake|Catherine Blake]] struck down his lawsuit on May 20, 2020, saying that the state and country are "now in the grip of a public health crisis more severe than any seen for a hundred years."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Federal judge rejects Cox lawsuit over Hogan's COVID-19 executive orders |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/coronavirus/federal-judge-rejects-cox-lawsuit-over-hogans-covid-19-executive-orders/article_1723a534-fc00-5643-8b40-c3fa4402640a.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=May 20, 2020}}</ref> He withdrew his request for a temporary restraining order on July 20, 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Del. Cox withdraws restraining order, injunction request from appeals court |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/coronavirus/del-cox-withdraws-restraining-order-injunction-request-from-appeals-court/article_2fb433b9-e121-5067-9051-1010ad609696.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=July 20, 2020}}</ref> Blake again rejected another lawsuit against COVID-19 restrictions filed by Cox in November 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |title=Federal Judge Dismisses Case Challenging Maryland's Pandemic-Related Restrictions |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/blog/federal-judge-dismisses-case-challenging-marylands-pandemic-related-restrictions/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=November 18, 2020}}</ref> In December 2020, Montgomery County Circuit Court Judge James Bonifant rejected a request made by local restaurants represented by Cox and attorney Ed Hartman to reverse an executive order issued by County Executive [[Marc Elrich]] that prohibited indoor dining at restaurants.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=County Circuit Courts Uphold Officials' Decisions to Ban Indoor Dining |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/blog/county-circuit-courts-uphold-officials-decisions-to-ban-indoor-dining/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=December 23, 2020}}</ref>

In May 2020, Cox attended a rally in [[Annapolis, Maryland|Annapolis]] which protested Hogan's stay-at-home orders alongside delegates [[Warren E. Miller]] and [[Brian Chisholm (politician)|Brian Chisholm]], former delegate [[Deb Rey]], [[2020 Maryland's 7th congressional district special election|7th congressional district special election]] candidate Liz Matory, [[Maryland's 2nd congressional district|2nd congressional district]] candidate Tim Fazenbaker, [[WalkAway campaign|#WalkAway]] founder [[Brandon Straka]], and [[2022 Maryland gubernatorial election|2022 gubernatorial candidate]] and [[perennial candidate]] [[Robin Ficker]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Protesters at Annapolis Reopen Rally Call for an End to 'Tyranny' |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2020/05/15/protesters-at-annapolis-reopen-rally-call-for-an-end-to-tyranny/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=May 15, 2020}}</ref> Later in the month, Cox posted a tweet promoting a [[conspiracy theory]] linking the [[Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation]] to the coronavirus pandemic.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Domen |first1=John |title=Maryland Del. Cox appears to promote Bill Gates coronavirus conspiracy theory |url=https://wtop.com/maryland/2020/05/md-delegate-appears-to-promote-bill-gates-conspiracy-theory/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=May 11, 2020}}</ref>

In October 2020, Cox provided legal representation for a [[Harford County, Maryland|Harford County]] man who was arrested for not wearing a mask at a polling place, arguing that the pair were only ordered to wear masks once they were inside the facility and election staff learned that they were Republicans.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leckrone |first1=Bennett |title=Lawmaker Takes Legal Case of Man Arrested for Not Wearing a Mask at Polling Place |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/blog/gop-lawmaker-takes-legal-case-of-man-arrested-for-not-wearing-a-mask-at-polling-place/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=October 28, 2020}}</ref> The family's request for a temporary restraining order to allow them to vote without wearing masks was rejected by Harford County Circuit Judge Angela M. Eaves.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Opilo |first1=Emily |title=Judge rejects request from Harford County father and son to vote without face masks |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2020/10/30/judge-rejects-request-from-harford-county-father-and-son-to-vote-without-face-masks/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=October 30, 2020}}</ref>

In January 2021, Cox cosponsored the Consent of the Governed Act, which would remove the governor's ability to unilaterally declare a state of emergency and would require the governor to form a special session to extend any state of emergency declaration for longer than fourteen days, which would require two-thirds approval from both chambers of the [[Maryland General Assembly]].<ref>{{cite news |title=A Republican Maryland Delegate Wants To Limit Hogan's Emergency Powers |url=https://dcist.com/story/21/01/29/republican-maryland-delegate-wants-to-limit-hogans-emergency-powers/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[DCist]] |date=January 29, 2021 |archive-date=March 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308145659/https://dcist.com/story/21/01/29/republican-maryland-delegate-wants-to-limit-hogans-emergency-powers/ |url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2021, Cox proposed a resolution that would immediately end Hogan's [[COVID-19|coronavirus]] emergency declaration.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=Cox Resolution Would End Covid Emergency in Md. |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/02/22/cox-resolution-would-end-covid-emergency-in-md/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 22, 2021}}</ref> In March 2021, he introduced a bill that would ban any requirement to show proof of "a medical examination, a vaccination, a medical test, or any other medical information" for employment or travel, and would allow parents to object to a child's vaccination as a requirement to be admitted to a public school.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Olson |first1=Laura |title='Vaccine Passports' That Show You're Inoculated Are On the Way |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/03/17/vaccine-passports-that-show-youre-inoculated-are-on-the-way/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=March 17, 2021}}</ref>

In June 2021, Cox called on Hogan to end the state's coronavirus emergency declaration.<ref>{{cite news |title=Frederick County Delegate Calls On Governor To End Maryland's State Of Emergency |url=https://www.wfmd.com/2021/06/10/frederick-county-delegate-calls-on-governor-to-end-marylands-state-of-emergency/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[WFMD]] |date=June 10, 2021}}</ref> In August 2021, Cox used his Facebook page to ask his constituents to e-mail the [[Maryland State Department of Education|Maryland Board of Education]] to demand that they reject an emergency universal masking mandate regulation for the 2021–2022 academic year.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=State Board of Education Approves Universal Masking in Schools |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/08/26/state-board-of-education-approves-universal-masking-in-schools/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=August 26, 2021}}</ref>

Cox claims to have survived COVID-19 twice, saying that during one of his bouts with the virus, he took [[hydroxychloroquine]] and [[ivermectin]] he obtained in Florida.<ref name="Banner bio" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jones |first1=Natalie |title=Medical freedom conference criticizes vaccine mandates, lack of hospital staffing |url=https://www.stardem.com/news/local_news/medical-freedom-conference-criticizes-vaccine-mandates-lack-of-hospital-staffing/article_ac183804-ea27-5859-ae97-4bb0eb14656c.html |access-date=December 16, 2021 |work=[[The Star Democrat]] |date=December 15, 2021}}</ref> Currently, ivermectin is used to treat parasites in livestock and river blindness in humans. It is of no benefit in preventing or treating [[COVID-19]].<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Popp M, Reis S, Schießer S, Hausinger RI, Stegemann M, Metzendorf MI, Kranke P, Meybohm P, Skoetz N, Weibel S |title=Ivermectin for preventing and treating COVID-19 |journal=Cochrane Database Syst Rev |volume=2022 |issue= 6|article-number=CD015017 |date=June 2022 |pmid=35726131 |pmc=9215332 |doi=10.1002/14651858.CD015017.pub3 |type=Systematic review |display-authors=5}}</ref>

In January 2022, Cox attended a rally against [[vaccine mandates]] in Annapolis, Maryland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaines |first1=Danielle E. |title=A Mixture of Hope and Solemnity as General Assembly Gets to Work |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/01/12/a-mixture-of-hope-and-solemnity-as-general-assembly-gets-to-work/ |access-date=January 13, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=January 12, 2022}}</ref> In September 2022, Cox said that he was "pro-vaccine" but does not believe in mandates.<ref name="MSUforum">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Cox faces tough questions at his solo Morgan State gubernatorial forum |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/27/maryland-gubernatorial-candidate-dan-cox-faces-tough-questions-at-his-solo-morgan-state-gubernatorial-forum/ |access-date=September 27, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 27, 2022}}</ref>

In February 2022, Cox introduced articles of impeachment against Governor Larry Hogan for his handling of the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |title=Gov. Larry Hogan faces impeachment call from Trump-aligned Republican |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/02/10/larry-hogan-impeachment/ |access-date=February 10, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=February 10, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=In Longshot Move, Del. Cox Seeks to Impeach Gov. Hogan |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/02/10/in-longshot-move-del-cox-seeks-to-impeach-gov-hogan/ |access-date=February 10, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=February 10, 2022}}</ref> It was the first serious effort to impeach a Maryland governor in the state's history.<ref name="Backbench" /> The Maryland House Rules and Executive Nominations Committee voted unanimously to reject the articles of impeachment.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=House Rules Panel Rejects Cox's Attempts to Impeach Hogan |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/03/03/house-rules-panel-rejects-coxs-attempts-to-impeach-hogan/ |access-date=March 3, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=March 3, 2022}}</ref>

===Crime and policing===
During the 2019 legislative session, Cox introduced a bill to create a task force to study the history of criminal and civil classifications in Maryland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Hough, Cox team up to advance sentencing reform |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/courts/hough-cox-team-up-to-advance-sentencing-reform/article_863a1211-afd2-5672-8196-aec4efd926c6.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=February 19, 2019}}</ref> He also supported a bill that would require people convicted of a violent crime to register information and a photo with the state, and opposed a bill prohibiting people convicted of animal abuse from adopting or buying another pet.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Defeated but undaunted: Legislators leave losing legislation for next year |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/levels_of_government/state/defeated-but-undaunted-legislators-leave-losing-legislation-for-next-year/article_27a08291-e7ff-5556-9347-85fecc13c5c6.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=March 6, 2019}}</ref>

In June 2022, Cox released a crime plan on [[Truth Social]] that called for allowing "modified [[stop and frisk]]" policies and enacting [[broken windows policing]]. In July, after a [[Squeegee man|squeegee worker]] fatally shot a driver wielding a baseball bat, he posted on the conservative social media platform [[Gettr]] that he would "target an end of squeegee crimes and all crime no matter how small" and pledged to remove [[Marilyn Mosby]] as Baltimore State's Attorney.<ref name="CoxCrime" /> Cox has expressed interest in establishing a "prosecutorial board" to work with elected leaders in Baltimore and other crime-challenged areas to keep violent criminals in prisons. He also said he was open to using a [[receivership]] to take control of Baltimore to help combat rising crime.<ref name="CoxWT" /><ref name="CoxCrime">{{cite news |last1=Opilo |first1=Emily |title=Moore pledges cooperation; Cox to take Baltimore by court order. What path does city take under Maryland's next governor? |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/23/moore-pledges-cooperation-cox-to-take-baltimore-by-court-order-what-path-does-city-take-under-marylands-next-governor/ |access-date=September 23, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 23, 2022}}</ref> In an interview with ''[[WDCW|DC News Now]]'', Cox said he would provide law enforcement officers with [[qualified immunity]] protections along with increased pay.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fleming |first1=Leonard |title=Maryland gubernatorial hopefuls debate crime and solutions to keep and recruit officers |url=https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/politics/election-maryland/maryland-gubernatorial-hopefuls-debate-crime-and-solutions-to-keep-and-recruit-officers/ |access-date=September 16, 2022 |work=[[WDCW]] |date=September 16, 2022 |archive-date=September 17, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220917014953/https://www.dcnewsnow.com/news/politics/election-maryland/maryland-gubernatorial-hopefuls-debate-crime-and-solutions-to-keep-and-recruit-officers/}}</ref>

Following the August 8, 2022 [[FBI search of Mar-a-Lago]], Cox said on social media that he would use the [[Maryland State Police]] and [[Maryland National Guard]] to "stand against" the federal government.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |title=Dan Cox says he would use Maryland law enforcement to 'stand against' federal government after raid on Trump's residence |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/08/09/dan-cox-says-he-would-use-maryland-law-enforcement-to-stand-against-federal-government-after-raid-on-trumps-residence/ |access-date=August 9, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=August 9, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Md. governor hopeful Dan Cox says he would use state police against Biden |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/08/09/dan-cox-trump-search-maryland-governor/?tid=ss_tw |access-date=August 9, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=August 9, 2022}}</ref> He also compared the FBI search to the actions of the [[Stasi]], [[East Germany]]'s secret police.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jiménez |first1=Miguel |title=Why the Democrats supported extreme Trumpists in the Republican primaries |url=https://english.elpais.com/usa/2022-09-16/why-the-democrats-supported-extreme-trumpists-in-the-republican-primaries.html |access-date=September 16, 2022 |work=[[El País]] |date=September 16, 2022}}</ref> At a campaign rally later that month, Cox said he opposed attacks on law enforcement officers, saying, "It's the politicians that matter. It's not the law enforcement. It's the orders from the politicians. We have to make sure we're not threatening the law enforcement officers. It's the politicians who are giving the orders. It's the politicians who have to go."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kurtz |first1=Josh |title=At campaign HQ opening, Cox calls Moore a communist, modulates rhetoric on FBI search of Mar-a-Lago |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2022/08/15/at-campaign-hq-opening-cox-calls-moore-a-communist-modulates-rhetoric-on-fbi-search-of-mar-a-lago/ |access-date=August 15, 2022 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=August 15, 2022}}</ref>

During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, Cox supported banning [[no-knock warrant]]s, citing the [[Fourth Amendment to the United States Constitution]].<ref name="CoxAffordability" />

===Education===
As a state delegate, Cox was a vocal critic of the [[Blueprint for Maryland's Future]], a sweeping education reform package passed by the Maryland General Assembly in 2021. During the 2022 legislative session, Cox introduced the Maryland Parent Rights Act (HB0618), a bill that would have allowed parents a larger role in their kids' education including notifying them when curriculum changes had been made. The bill failed to pass out of committee.<ref name="Linthicum">{{cite news |last1=Munro |first1=Dana |title=GOP governor candidate Dan Cox promises to cut taxes, reduce crime, give parents a voice in education at Linthicum meeting |url=https://www.capitalgazette.com/politics/ac-cn-dan-cox-comes-to-anne-arundel-20221007-aaov3xcwhrakbmnrdhe35l5pp4-story.html |access-date=October 7, 2022 |work=[[Capital Gazette]] |date=October 7, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Legislation - HB0618 |url=https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb0618?ys=2022RS |website=mgaleg.maryland.gov |publisher=[[Maryland General Assembly]] |access-date=October 7, 2022}}</ref> He also voted in favor of an amendment introduced by state Delegate [[Kathy Szeliga]] that would have blocked public schools from discussing gender and sexuality in the classroom.<ref name="MooreCox Sun" />

During his 2022 gubernatorial campaign, Cox unveiled a "Defending Parental Rights" education platform that would ban teaching children in kindergarten through third grade about [[gender identity]], calling it "indoctrination."<ref name="EducationCox">{{cite news |last1=Reed |first1=Lillian |title=Maryland gubernatorial candidates Dan Cox, Wes Moore court parents, teachers, voters with education a key part of both their platforms |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/21/maryland-gubernatorial-candidates-dan-cox-wes-moore-court-parents-teachers-voters-with-education-a-key-part-of-both-their-platforms/ |access-date=September 16, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 16, 2022}}</ref> When asked to elaborate his definition of indoctrination, he cited asexual and nonbinary author [[Maia Kobabe]]'s memoirs ''[[Gender Queer]]'', which he claimed depicts "things that I cannot show you on television, it's so disgusting."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lavers |first1=Michael |title=Cox criticizes 'transgender indoctrination' in Md. kindergartens |url=https://www.washingtonblade.com/2022/10/13/cox-criticizes-transgender-indoctrination-in-md-kindergartens/ |access-date=October 15, 2022 |work=[[The Washington Blade]] |date=October 13, 2022}}</ref> Kobabe's book is not being taught in any kindergarten or elementary school classes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Russell |first1=John |title=GOP candidate accuses opponent of wanting "transgender indoctrination in kindergarten" |url=https://www.lgbtqnation.com/2022/10/gop-candidate-accuses-opponent-wanting-transgender-indoctrination-kindergarten/ |access-date=October 15, 2022 |work=[[LGBTQ Nation]] |date=October 14, 2022}}</ref> He's also called for a ban on [[critical race theory]], which is not taught in Maryland public schools, and supports the expansion of [[school choice]] by increasing funding for the state's BOOST program, which provides low-income families with scholarships to attend charter schools. In September 2022, Cox said he would appoint more parents to the [[Maryland State Department of Education|Maryland State Board of Education]].<ref name="EducationCox" /> In October 2022, Cox promised to create an office for parents' rights on his first day in office<ref name="Linthicum" /> and said that he supported mandating agriculture education in schools.<ref name="Lancaster" /> During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, Cox declined to say whether he believed kindergartners were subject to indoctrination in public schools regarding gender policy.<ref name="debate2026">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=MD gubernatorial debate: Wes Moore, Dan Cox trade barbs but deflect questions |url=https://thedailyrecord.com/2026/10/06/governor-debate-wes-moore-dan-cox/ |access-date=October 6, 2026 |work=The Daily Record |date=October 6, 2026}}</ref>

During his 2026 gubernatorial election, Cox supported conducting an independent financial audit of Maryland's public education system.<ref name="MeetEight">{{cite news |last1=Petrowich |first1=Sarah |title=Meet the eight Maryland Republicans vying to thwart Gov. Wes Moore's reelection bid |url=https://www.wypr.org/wypr-news/2026-06-04/meet-the-eight-maryland-republicans-vying-to-thwart-gov-wes-moores-reelection-bid |access-date=June 4, 2026 |work=[[WYPR]] |date=June 4, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> He also supported increasing parental involvement in public education and placing accountability measures on the Blueprint for Maryland's Future.<ref name="CoxAffordability" />

===Gun policy===
In February 2018, following the [[Parkland high school shooting]], Cox said he opposed laws that ban assault weapons and restrict gun ownership, instead endorsing legislation to [[arm teachers]] and [[school resource officer]]s.<ref name="Cox2018" />

In 2019, Cox said he opposed a bill to abolish the state's Handgun Permit Review Board.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Lawmakers question if handgun permit board decisions were improper |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/levels_of_government/state/delegates/lawmakers-question-if-handgun-permit-board-decisions-were-improper/article_ef6f7df1-5325-5ece-85bf-a7e33fc41bc1.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=April 2, 2019}}</ref>

During the 2022 legislative session, Cox opposed a bill that would ban the possession and sale of [[privately made firearm]]s, which he compared to the [[2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine]]. He also voted against legislation that would increase security measures at firearms stores. In June 2022, Cox celebrated the Supreme Court's ruling in ''[[New York State Rifle & Pistol Association, Inc. v. Bruen]]''.<ref name="MooreCox Sun">{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |last2=Janesch |first2=Sam |title=Here's where Wes Moore and Dan Cox stand on health care, guns, the environment and more |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/06/heres-where-wes-moore-and-dan-cox-stand-on-health-care-guns-the-environment-and-more/ |access-date=September 6, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 6, 2022}}</ref>

===Healthcare===
During his 2016 House of Representatives campaign, Cox said that he would move to scrap the [[Affordable Care Act]] if elected.<ref name="Nirappil">{{cite news |last1=Nirappil |first1=Fenit |last2=Turque |first2=Bill |title=GOP primary in Md.'s 8th District: 4 moderates and a conservative |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/md-politics/gop-primary-in-mds-8th-district-4-moderates-and-a-conservative/2016/04/16/c0ff5618-0016-11e6-9d36-33d198ea26c5_story.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=April 16, 2016}}</ref>

During the 2019 legislative session, Cox voted against the End-of-Life Option Act, which would have provided [[palliative care]] to terminally ill adults.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Md. House narrowly passes bill allowing medical aid in dying |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/rights/md-house-narrowly-passes-bill-allowing-medical-aid-in-dying/article_460eb57a-66fa-536f-b367-32bf19ecc670.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=March 7, 2019}}</ref>

On [[Holocaust Remembrance Day]] in April 2021, Cox said he would vote against a bill to allow minors to consent to some health care services, comparing it to the infringement of "the rights of parents" by Nazis.<ref name="JTA">{{cite news |title=GOP lawmaker marks Holocaust remembrance day by likening child mental health bill to Nazi laws |url=https://www.jta.org/quick-reads/gop-lawmaker-marks-holocaust-remembrance-day-by-likening-child-mental-health-bill-to-nazi-laws |access-date=April 16, 2021 |work=Jewish Telegraphic Agency |date=April 8, 2021}}</ref> Cox's [[Nazi analogies]] were criticized by the local [[Jewish Community Relations Council]].<ref name="JTA" /> Cox defended his remarks in a letter accusing his colleagues of twisting the words he used during an emotionally-charged floor debate to gain partisan advantage, and offered no apology for his comments.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DePuyt |first1=Bruce |title=In Letter, Del. Cox Defends Remarks Invoking Holocaust on House Floor |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/04/27/in-letter-del-cox-defends-remarks-invoking-holocaust-on-house-floor/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=April 27, 2021}}</ref>

===Immigration===
During his 2016 House of Representatives campaign, Cox strongly rallied on regulating immigration. He said that he would support the full enforcement of existing laws and passage of [[Kate's Law]], which would establish mandatory minimum five-year prison sentences for any immigrant convicted of reentering the country after being deported.<ref name="Nirappil" />

Cox opposed HB892,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/legislation/details/hb0892?ys=2020RS |title=Legislation - HB0892 |website=Maryland General Assembly |date=September 22, 2020}}</ref> a bill that would require a [[search warrant|warrant]] in order for the [[U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] to search through the state's driver's license database.<ref>{{cite news |title=Md. Legislators Considering An Immigration Enforcement Restrictions Bill |url=https://www.wfmd.com/2020/02/11/md-legislators-considering-an-immigration-enforcement-restrictions-bill/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[WFMD]] |date=February 11, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Iannelli |first1=Nick |title=Md. lawmakers may limit ICE's power, despite threat from Trump administration |url=https://wtop.com/maryland/2020/02/despite-threat-from-trump-administration-md-may-limit-ices-power/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=February 27, 2020}}</ref> He also opposed the Maryland Highway Safety Act of 2013, which allowed undocumented immigrants to apply for driver's licenses.<ref name="Cox2018" />

Cox strongly opposed the Dignity Not Detention Act, which would have required people arrested for federal civil crimes to be detained in federal facilities rather than in state or local facilities. During the House debate before the bill's final vote, Cox read off a list of offenses that local law enforcement agencies would not be able to use to pass undocumented people over to ICE after they completed sentences for their crimes. The bill passed the Maryland General Assembly with a veto-proof majority, but was vetoed by Governor Hogan on May 27, 2021.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gaskill |first1=Hannah |title=Legislature Passes Immigrants' Rights Bill; Hogan Veto Expected |url=https://www.marylandmatters.org/2021/04/13/legislature-passes-immigrants-rights-bill-hogan-veto-expected/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Maryland Matters]] |date=April 13, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Velazquez |first1=Rose |title=Hogan vetoes bill that would have ended Worcester County contract to house ICE detainees |url=https://www.delmarvanow.com/story/news/local/maryland/2021/05/27/gov-hogan-vetoes-dignity-not-detention-act-house-bill-16/7454969002/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[The Daily Times (Salisbury, Maryland)|The Daily Times]] |location=[[Salisbury, Maryland]] |date=May 27, 2021}}</ref>

===Marijuana===
In 2021, Cox voted against a bill to decriminalize the possession of [[drug paraphernalia]]. In 2022, Cox voted against legislation that would create a ballot referendum to legalize recreational marijuana in Maryland, and another bill that would regulate marijuana possession should the referendum pass in November.<ref name="MooreCox Sun" /> During a debate on October 12, 2022, Cox said that he supported the release of those charged with the possession of small amounts of marijuana.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McQueen |first1=Tashi |title=Wes Moore and Dan Cox debate the topics: abortion, Jan 6, the racial wealth gap and education |url=https://afro.com/wes-moore-and-dan-cox-debate-the-topics-abortion-jan-6-the-racial-wealth-gap-and-education/ |access-date=October 19, 2022 |work=[[Baltimore Afro-American]] |date=October 18, 2022}}</ref>

===QAnon===
In October 2020, Cox made a post on his Twitter account that contained [[hashtags]] related to the [[QAnon conspiracy theory]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lublin |first1=David |title=Republican Del. Dan Cox Now Using QAnon Hashtags |url=https://www.theseventhstate.com/?p=14058 |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=Seventh State |date=October 24, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Political notes: Del. Cox tweets QAnon hashtag, a theory that's been widely debunked |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/terms/printview/local/political_notes/political-notes-del-cox-tweets-qanon-hashtag-a-theory-thats-been-widely-debunked/article_40e18df6-75c0-575d-81a9-4fea68485079.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=October 28, 2020}}</ref>

In April 2022, Cox attended "Patriots Arise for God and Country" conference in [[Gettysburg, Pennsylvania]]. The event was organized by QAnon conspiracy theorists Francine and Allen Fodsick and featured images of conspiracy theories related to the [[9/11 conspiracy theories|September 11 attacks]], the [[John F. Kennedy assassination conspiracy theories|assassination of John F. Kennedy]], and [[Vaccine misinformation|vaccines]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Seidman |first1=Andrew |title=A top GOP candidate for governor campaigned at an event promoting QAnon and conspiracy theories about 9/11 |url=https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/doug-mastriano-teddy-daniels-qanon-conference-gop-candidates-20220427.html |access-date=April 28, 2022 |work=[[The Philadelphia Inquirer]] |date=April 27, 2022}}</ref>

In June 2022, Cox ambushed a rally for gubernatorial candidate [[Kelly M. Schulz|Kelly Schulz]], where Governor [[Larry Hogan]] called him a "QAnon conspiracy theorist". In response, Cox yelled out, "Defamation, sir!" When asked to elaborate on how he had been defamed, Cox said that he was not a member of QAnon.<ref name="SchulzPush" /> In October 2022, when asked by [[WJZ-TV]] if he believed in any of QAnon's beliefs, Cox replied, "Absolutely not. I'm disavowing all of that. This is just a lie."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gessler |first1=Paul |title=Republican Dan Cox - endorsed by Trump - has focus on Maryland governor's race |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/republican-dan-cox-endorsed-by-trump-has-focus-on-maryland-governors-race/ |access-date=October 15, 2022 |work=[[WJZ-TV]] |date=October 14, 2022}}</ref>

===Social issues===
In April 2001, Cox and his wife Valerie wrote a letter to ''The Dorchester Star'' about the state's Administration Act of 2001 (SB 205), which would ban discrimination against people based on their sexual identity. In the letter, they argued that the bill would violate the rights of "business owners ... who firmly believe homosexuality is sin and those who practice it are in danger of temporal disease and eternal death."<ref name="Upended" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Cambridge Reader opposes Bill SB 205 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/dorchester-star-dan-coxs-anti-gay-right/154287183/ |access-date=October 15, 2022 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |newspaper=Dorchester Star |date=April 20, 2001 |location=[[Cambridge, Maryland]] |page=4}}</ref>

During his 2016 House of Representatives campaign, Cox said that he did not support an increase in the [[Minimum wage in the United States|minimum wage]].<ref name="fnp interview 2016" /> During the 2019 legislative session, he testified against a bill to raise Maryland's minimum wage to [[Fight for $15|$15 an hour]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Samantha |title=Busy legislative week culminates in votes on minimum wage, right-to-die bills |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/levels_of_government/state/delegates/busy-legislative-week-culminates-in-votes-on-minimum-wage-right-to-die-bills/article_3e002639-a97d-59d2-951f-6bcafb6afb06.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=March 2, 2019}}</ref>

In August 2017, Cox served as the attorney in a lawsuit challenging the [[Frederick County Public Schools (Maryland)|Frederick County Public School]]'s policy on [[transgender]] students.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Etzler |first1=Allen |title=Lawsuit filed over Frederick County Public Schools' transgender policy |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/education/lawsuit-filed-over-frederick-county-public-schools-transgender-policy/article_afc18468-0b87-5308-961e-87cdeb5d0440.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=August 14, 2017}}</ref> The plaintiffs dropped the lawsuit on November 28, 2017, citing stress and potential humiliation that stemmed from the legal action.<ref>{{cite news |last1=St. George |first1=Donna |title=Legal challenge to transgender policy in Maryland school system withdrawn |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/education/legal-challenge-to-transgender-policy-in-maryland-school-system-withdrawn/2017/11/28/54c95cf8-d3a7-11e7-a986-d0a9770d9a3e_story.html |access-date=November 23, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=November 28, 2017}}</ref> In July 2022, Cox said that he would ban transgender students from competing on girls' sports team in schools.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |last2=Wiggins |first2=Ovetta |title=Wes Moore vs. Dan Cox: A fight for Md. governor that will echo downballot |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/07/23/maryland-governor-moore-cox/ |access-date=July 23, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=July 23, 2022}}</ref>

In September 2018, Cox disputed the [[Brett Kavanaugh Supreme Court nomination#Sexual assault allegations|sexual assault accusations]] made against Supreme Court nominee [[Brett Kavanaugh]], claiming that his accusers "withheld evidence" from the confirmation hearing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Political Notes: Delauter defends 287(g) program amid continued concerns from residents |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/politics_and_government/levels_of_government/county/council/political-notes-delauter-defends-287-g-program-amid-continued-concerns-from-residents/article_2f32bd4c-97fb-504b-9c99-c8c1a2ac816d.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=September 27, 2018}}</ref>

In September 2019, Cox introduced a bill to add "dignity of the human body" to the state's [[Hate crime laws in the United States|hate crime]] statute to cover spitting on the victim.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=David |title=Frederick County delegate is drafting legislation to expand hate crime definition |url=https://www.wbaltv.com/article/delegate-dan-cox-is-drafting-legislation-to-expand-hate-crime-definition/29233664 |access-date=February 7, 2020 |work=[[WBAL-TV]] |date=September 25, 2019 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Broom |first1=Scott |title=Should spitting on a victim be a hate crime? |url=https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/crime/frederick-hate-crime/65-605730b1-5ca8-4ed8-b111-b12918117288 |access-date=February 7, 2020 |work=[[WUSA (TV)]] |date=September 25, 2019}}</ref> The bill was named for John Weed, a Frederick County man who died after being assaulted and spit on.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bohnel |first1=Steve |title=Del. Cox proposes changes to state hate crime law after assault at fair |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/crime_and_justice/del-cox-proposes-changes-to-state-hate-crime-law-after-assault-at-fair/article_160dc21e-9a14-5f5c-9e9e-6b6e2437975e.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=September 24, 2019}}</ref>

In March 2021, Cox voted against HB667, a bill that would abandon "[[Maryland, My Maryland]]" as Maryland's state song.<ref>{{cite web |title=Legislation - HB0667 |url=https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb0667?ys=2021RS |website=Maryland General Assembly |date=May 20, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Cohen |first1=Steven |title=Maryland decides to replace its state song |url=https://www.localdvm.com/news/maryland/maryland-decides-to-replace-its-state-song/ |access-date=November 23, 2021 |work=[[WDVM-TV]] |date=March 31, 2021 |archive-date=April 1, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210401024321/https://www.localdvm.com/news/maryland/maryland-decides-to-replace-its-state-song/}}</ref>

In 2022, Cox was one of 21 legislators to vote against a bill that would raise the state's minimum marriage age from 15 to 17, saying in an interview that a pregnant 16-year-old should be allowed to marry the father.<ref name="Backbench" />

===Taxes===
During his 2016 House of Representatives campaign, Cox expressed support for [[trickle-down economics]] and called for "major cuts" to government spending.<ref name="Reaganomics">{{cite news |last1=Lavin |first1=Nancy |title=U.S. House race: Cox pledges to revive Reaganomic policies |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/continuing_coverage/election_2016/u-s-house-race-cox-pledges-to-revive-reaganomic-policies/article_eaff28e6-8b6e-5d3c-abbf-77c6fa60ee9d.html |access-date=October 9, 2023 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=February 16, 2016}}</ref>

During his 2022 gubernatorial campaign, Cox said he would immediately suspend the state's gas tax.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cox |first1=Erin |title=Surging gas taxes expected to be political weapon in Maryland politics |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/05/26/maryland-gas-tax-politics/ |access-date=September 14, 2022 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=May 26, 2022}}</ref> He also said he would support providing businesses with increased tax credits, including a "dollar-for-dollar" tax credit to help employers cover the costs of paying for [[paid leave|paid family and medical leave]]. Cox also supports eliminating the state income tax and would support cutting the state's corporate tax rate, personal income tax rate,<ref name="CoxWT">{{cite news |last1=McLaughlin |first1=Seth |title=Republican Dan Cox laser-focused on the economy, crime and education in Maryland governor's race |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2022/sep/14/dan-cox-laser-focused-economy-crime-and-education-/ |access-date=September 14, 2022 |work=[[The Washington Times]] |date=September 14, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=Maryland gubernatorial nominees Dan Cox, Wes Moore talk child care, paid leave and budget surplus in virtual forum |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/09/14/maryland-gubernatorial-nominees-dan-cox-wes-moore-talk-child-care-paid-leave-and-budget-surplus-in-virtual-forum/ |access-date=September 14, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=September 14, 2022}}</ref> and property tax rates.<ref name="Lancaster">{{cite news |last1=Gruber |first1=Philip |title=Where Do Maryland Governor Candidates Stand on Ag Issues? |url=https://www.lancasterfarming.com/farming-news/news/where-do-maryland-governor-candidates-stand-on-ag-issues/article_9ba77260-40ba-11ed-826b-8bc470d6f35e.html |access-date=October 8, 2022 |work=[[Lancaster Farming]] |date=October 8, 2022}}</ref><ref name="Linthicum" />

During his 2026 gubernatorial campaign, Cox supported repealing tax increases passed during Moore's first term, rolling back property tax assessment valuation increases,<ref name="CoxAffordability" /> and cutting the state's corporate tax rate from 8.25% to 5.5%.<ref name="WBAL2026" /> He also supported eliminating property taxes for homeowners 65 and older.<ref name="Foreback">{{cite news |last1=Foreback |first1=Caroline |title=Dan Cox outlines plans to make Maryland more affordable in bid to unseat Wes Moore |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/dan-cox-plans-maryland-governor-gubernatorial-affordable-wes-moore/ |access-date=October 19, 2026 |work=[[WJZ-TV]] |date=October 19, 2026}}</ref>

===Transportation===
[[File:Announcement of I270 Corridor Improvements (27793213624).jpg|thumb|Cox attends a I-270 press briefing with Governor Larry Hogan, 2016]]
During his 2016 House of Representatives campaign, Cox said he supported efforts to reduce congestion on [[Interstate 270 (Maryland)|Interstate 270]].<ref name="Reaganomics" /> During his 2022 gubernatorial campaign, he said he supported a proposal to expand I-270 and the [[Capital Beltway]], but opposed plans to add [[high-occupancy toll lane]]s or use [[public–private partnership]]s to fund the new lanes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moore |first1=Jack |title=Moore vs. Cox: Maryland governor candidates on economy, education, transportation |url=https://wtop.com/maryland/2022/11/moore-vs-cox-maryland-governor-candidates-on-economy-education-transportation/ |access-date=July 18, 2023 |work=[[WTOP-FM]] |date=November 3, 2022}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Cox married Valerie Denise Gilliard in 1996.<ref name="wedding" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Masters |first1=Kate |title=Beyond the Ballot — Dan Cox |url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/news/arts_and_entertainment/beyond-the-ballot-dan-cox/article_16727f28-4277-5b10-bf1b-135fbeab7cd1.html |access-date=July 20, 2022 |work=[[Frederick News-Post]] |date=February 28, 2019}}</ref> They have ten children.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Janesch |first1=Sam |title=What you need to know about Maryland GOP gubernatorial nominee Dan Cox |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/2022/07/20/what-you-need-to-know-about-maryland-gop-gubernatorial-nominee-dan-cox/ |access-date=July 20, 2022 |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |date=July 20, 2022}}</ref>

